export const lpContent={
 'sun':{
  title:'The Sun',one:'The day’s light and warmth — and how to enjoy sunshine while being kind to eyes and skin.',
  seoTitle:'Sun Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Sunshine, Warmth and Shadow Safety (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what the sun is and does, a warmth-and-shade comparison, shadow tracking and the never-stare rule taught kindly.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches what the sun actually does for us — light, warmth, growth and shadows — and builds the sun-respect habits that keep eyes and skin happy all summer.',
  what:'The sun is the star that lights our daytime and warms our world. It rises, crosses the sky and sets, and everything on Earth — plants, animals, people — arranges its day around its light.',
  mean:'Sunlight does three big jobs at child height: it lets us see, it warms the ground and the air, and it feeds the plants that become our food. Where sunlight falls, shadows form on the other side — the sun is always the reason for a shadow.',
  know:'Your child needs three suns: the friendly sun that warms and lights; the bright sun that must never be stared at because its light is too strong for our eyes; and the summer sun whose heat asks for a hat, water and shade.',
  why:'The sun is a child’s first astronomy and first safety lesson in one object. Pointing at the sun, tracking its shadows and feeling its warmth build real scientific reasoning — and respecting its brightness is a habit that protects eyesight for life.',
  dev:'Cause-and-effect thinking (sun out, shadows sharp), temperature vocabulary, time concepts through the sun’s daily journey, observation skills, and early self-care: hat, water and shade become the child’s own decisions.',
  vocab:'sun, sunshine, shadow, warm, bright, sunrise, sunset, shade',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is the sun. It gives us light to see and warmth to feel."\n2) Read the picture together: the guide points up at the sun in the sky, wears sunglasses, puts on a sun hat, rests under a tree and holds a thermometer that reads 30 degrees.\n3) Do the two-spot test: stand one moment in sunshine, one in shade. Which feels warmer? That warm feeling IS the sun.\n4) Teach the eye rule with respect, not fright: the sun is too bright to look at straight on — we admire it the way we admire a lamp, from the side, never staring.\n5) Finish with the sunny-day plan: hat on, water bottle filled, play in the shade when the sun is strongest. Act all three out right now.',
  say:'"The sun gives us light so our eyes can see and warmth so we are cosy. It helps the flowers grow tall. We love the sun from far away — we never stare at it, and on hot sunny days we wear our hats and drink water."',
  notice:'On this card a guide in a straw hat stands in a sunny park: he points toward the sun high in the blue sky, holds his hand out to notice the bright light, puts on dark sunglasses, places his hat on his head, sits resting in the shade of a big tree, and holds a white thermometer that reads 30°C. Every panel is a real sunny-day action your child can copy.',
  ask:'"What does the sun give us?" "Where does a shadow come from?" "Which spot feels warmer — the sunshine or the shade?" "What do we never do with our eyes at the sun?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The sun warms my face," "My shadow follows me because the sun is up," or the safety line: "I never look right at the sun — it is too bright."',
  real:'Chase shadows on the walk to preschool — long in the morning, stubby at midday, long again in the evening. Notice sun-warmed surfaces: the bench that bakes, the car door that glows, the sand at the park that heats through your shoes.',
  play:'Shadow tag in the garden: step on each other’s shadows instead of bodies. Then shadow freeze — when the sun "hides" behind a cloud, everyone freezes; when it peeks out, shadows dance again. Finish with a chalk line around a shadow and re-check it after lunch: it moved!',
  tips:'Model the eye rule yourself — if you squint and turn away from the sun, narrate it: "Too bright for my eyes, I will look down here." Children copy what you do with your face far faster than what you say. And keep hats by the door so grabbing one is automatic.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the sun give us? Where must we never look? What do we wear on a hot sunny day?" Light-plus-warmth, never-stare, hat-and-water — three hits and the lesson has landed.',
  safety:'Never look directly at the sun — not with eyes alone, not through sunglasses as a "test". On hot days: hat, water breaks and shade rests, sunscreen applied by a grown-up, and never wait inside a parked car, not even for a minute.'},
 'clouds':{
  title:'Clouds',one:'Floating bags of tiny water drops — how to read the sky’s softest weather flags.',
  seoTitle:'Clouds Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Sky-Watching and Light vs Dark Clouds (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what clouds are made of, how they move, light-versus-dark cloud reading and a cloud-diary practice for little sky scientists.',
  intro:'This lesson turns "what are clouds?" from a shrug into a skill: your child learns what clouds hold, how to tell a fine-day cloud from a rain cloud, and how to watch the sky like a little forecaster.',
  what:'Clouds are collections of tiny water drops floating high in the air — so light they drift, so many they shade whole cities. They are not smoke or candyfloss; they are water you can see from far away.',
  mean:'Every cloud is a weather flag. White, fluffy clouds usually travel with a fine day. Grey, heavy-looking clouds are carrying more water — often rain on the way. Watching clouds move and change is reading the sky’s own news.',
  know:'Your child needs three cloud ideas: clouds are made of tiny water drops; white puffy clouds usually mean a nice day while grey heavy ones hint at rain; and clouds drift and change shape because the wind pushes them along.',
  why:'Clouds are the perfect first forecast tool — visible, free, changing daily. Reading them builds comparison vocabulary (light/dark, big/small, high/low) and the scientist’s habit of gathering clues before announcing a conclusion.',
  dev:'Observation and comparison, early prediction ("grey cloud, maybe rain"), shape-naming and imagination through cloud figures, weather vocabulary, and patient watching — a full minute of quiet sky-watching is a real attention win at this age.',
  vocab:'cloud, fluffy, grey, drizzle, float, sky, wind, rain cloud',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Clouds are made of lots and lots of tiny drops of water, floating high above us."\n2) Read the picture: the meteorologist looks at cloud shapes through a magnifying glass, watches clouds move, compares light clouds with dark clouds on a chart, checks a weather vane, draws a cloud and reads the forecast on a screen.\n3) Do the light-versus-dark test on the real sky today: find a white cloud and a grey cloud. Which one might be carrying rain?\n4) Watch one cloud move for a slow count of ten — it shifts. Something invisible is pushing it: the wind.\n5) Start a cloud diary: each morning, one quick drawing of today’s clouds. After a week, look back and find the rainiest-looking page.',
  say:'"Clouds are tiny drops of water floating high in the sky. White fluffy clouds usually mean a lovely day. When the clouds turn grey and heavy, they might be full of rain. We can read the clouds like a book!"',
  notice:'On this card a meteorologist in a white lab coat works beside a big window: she studies cloud shapes through a magnifying glass beside a cloud-types poster, looks through binoculars, points at a chart comparing light clouds with dark clouds, holds a weather vane, sketches a cloud in a notepad and points at a forecast screen showing three days — partly sunny, cloudy, rainy.',
  ask:'"What are clouds made of?" "Which cloud looks like it carries rain?" "Can you see the clouds moving?" "What shape does that cloud look like to you?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Clouds are tiny drops of water," "That grey cloud might drop rain!" or proudly report a cloud shape: "That one is a dinosaur — now it is a boat!"',
  real:'Check the sky before dressing each morning and let the clouds help pick the coat. At the bus stop, find the fastest-moving cloud. Airport days and tall buildings give cloud-level views that make the floating obvious.',
  play:'Cloud charades: lie back and take turns "being" a cloud shape for the other to guess. Then the rain-cloud game — start as a small white puff, grow bigger and greyer as your child piles on cushions, and end with a gentle finger-rain drizzle all over the carpet.',
  tips:'Resist the urge to make cloud-watching a quiz — the magic is in the lying-back and the noticing. If your child invents cloud animals, join in happily; imagination is the doorway through which observation walks. Keep the diary drawings tiny — ten seconds each.',
  check:'Ask: "What are clouds made of? Which clouds might bring rain? What pushes the clouds along?" Drops, the grey ones, the wind — three answers, one little forecaster born.',
  safety:'Pick cloud-watching spots away from roads and water. On bright days, shade or a hat first — nobody should squint upward into glare. And if the sky turns properly dark and rumbling, that is a cloud lesson for another day: inside we go.'},
 'rain':{
  title:'Rain',one:'Water falling home from the clouds — the loop that drinks the garden and fills the puddles.',
  seoTitle:'Rain Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Water Loop and Rainy-Day Wonder (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: where rain comes from, the water loop in child words, a rain-measuring jar and how raindrops feel, sound and splash.',
  intro:'This lesson explains where rain actually comes from and what it does for the world, then turns the next shower into a science station — drops to feel, rain to hear and a jar that measures the sky.',
  what:'Rain is water falling from the clouds. Water in seas, rivers and puddles rises up as invisible vapour, gathers into clouds, and when the drops grow big and heavy — down they come as rain.',
  mean:'Rain is the world’s watering can. It drinks the gardens, fills the rivers and reservoirs, washes the air and the leaves, and tops up every puddle. Every drop is on a loop: ground to cloud to ground again, over and over.',
  know:'Your child needs three rain ideas: rain comes from water held in clouds; rain gives every plant and animal the water they need; and rainy days ask for rain gear — and offer puddles.',
  why:'Rain is the entry point to the water cycle, the science story your child will retell for years. It also carries emotional weather: some children find rain gloomy, some find it cosy. Welcoming both feelings teaches that all weather — and all moods — passes.',
  dev:'Circular cause-and-effect (the water loop), sensory vocabulary for sound and touch, care for living things (the garden drinks too), patient measuring, and the dressing-sequencing skills that rainy mornings demand.',
  vocab:'rain, raindrop, puddle, splash, soak, shower, umbrella, wellies',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Rain is water falling from the clouds — the clouds are giving the world a drink."\n2) Read the picture: the girl feels raindrops land on her hand, listens to the rain with her eyes closed, collects rainwater in a big blue barrel from a pipe, wears a yellow raincoat, points at a puddle and checks a forecast that reads 18 degrees.\n3) Trace the water loop with your finger in the air: puddle → up to the cloud → rain → puddle again. It never ends!\n4) Catch the next rain on the doorstep: feel a drop on a palm, listen to it on the hood, watch it bead and run off the raincoat — the coat works!\n5) Put a jar outside before bed and measure the catch in the morning: that much sky-water fell while you slept.',
  say:'"Rain is how the clouds water the whole world — the trees, the flowers, you and me. Water goes up to make clouds, then falls back down as rain, then goes up again. On rainy days we wear our raincoat and boots, and the puddles are waiting."',
  notice:'On this card a girl in a green hat and green overalls stands in a rainy garden: she holds her palm out to feel the raindrops, cups her ear to listen with eyes closed, watches rainwater pour from a big pipe into a blue barrel, wears a yellow hooded raincoat, crouches to point at a puddle and holds a tablet showing a rain-cloud forecast at 18°C.',
  ask:'"Where does rain come from?" "Who drinks the rain?" "What do we put on before a rainy walk?" "What happens to puddles when the sun comes back?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Rain comes from the clouds," "The garden is drinking!" or the dressing report: "Raincoat zipped, hood up, boots on — ready!"',
  real:'Watch rain land in the birdbath, the sandpit cover and the watering can. Visit a rain barrel at a community garden. Notice which things love rain (worms, washed cars, ducks) and which hide from it (cats, picnics, chalk drawings).',
  play:'Puddle symphony: tap a tray of water slowly, then fast, then drum the rim — which sounds like today’s rain? Then the water-loop pantomime: everyone crouches as a "puddle", rises as vapour, huddles as a cloud, and sprinkle-falls back down as rain.',
  tips:'Do not rush to comfort a child who grumbles at rain — hand them the measuring jar or the listening game instead, and let the rain become interesting before it becomes background. And own the gear: a child who picked their own wellies begs for rain.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does rain come from? What does rain give the garden? What do we wear in the rain?" Clouds, a drink, raincoat-and-boots — the loop is learned.',
  safety:'Rain makes slopes, painted lines and leaves slippery — walking feet near roads, always. Wet roads make cars slower to stop, so hands are held at crossings. And when thunder joins the rain, the lesson shifts to storm rules: inside, cosy, away from windows.'},
 'rainbow':{
  title:'Rainbow',one:'Seven colours painted by sun plus rain — the sky’s shortest, happiest show.',
  seoTitle:'Rainbow Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Seven Colours and the Sun-plus-Rain Trick (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how sunlight through raindrops makes a rainbow, naming all seven colours in order, a mist-spray rainbow and rainbow-art practice.',
  intro:'This lesson explains the rainbow’s two ingredients — sunshine and raindrops — walks the seven colours in order, and gives your family the mist-spray trick that puts a rainbow in your own hands.',
  what:'A rainbow is a giant arc of coloured light. It appears when the sun shines through falling raindrops: each drop splits the white light into its colours, and together the drops paint the arc across the sky.',
  mean:'A rainbow needs both of its ingredients at once: light from behind you, rain in front of you. That is why rainbows visit right after showers and fade when the drops dry. The colours always arrive in the same order: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet.',
  know:'Your child needs three rainbow ideas: rainbows come from sun plus rain; the colours follow a fixed seven-colour order; and rainbows are quick visitors — enjoy them now, draw them later.',
  why:'A rainbow bundles colour knowledge, sequencing and the first optics into one unforgettable sight. Naming seven colours in order is genuine memorisation practice — and the brief visit teaches, gently, that some wonderful things do not last, which is a big feeling made safe.',
  dev:'Colour naming and ordering, sequential memory, light-and-water science, the emotional literacy of enjoying-and-letting-go, and art transfer — drawing the rainbow afterwards keeps both the colours and the memory.',
  vocab:'rainbow, arc, colours, light, raindrops, prism, fade, sky',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "When the sun shines through raindrops, the light splits into its colours — a rainbow!"\n2) Read the picture: the scientist spots a rainbow over a house, crouches by a puddle with a special glass that splits light, points at a chart naming all seven colours, shades her eyes from the sun, draws a rainbow with crayons and stands between two thought bubbles — a rain cloud and a smiling sun.\n3) Chant the seven colours with finger-counts: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet. Again, faster. Again, whispering.\n4) Teach the recipe like a cake: sunshine behind you + raindrops ahead = rainbow. Both, same time, that is the whole trick.\n5) Try the kitchen version on a sunny day: fine water mist from a hose or spray bottle, sun behind you — hunt the little rainbow hiding in the spray.',
  say:'"A rainbow comes when sun and rain are both in the sky. The sunbeam dives into the raindrops and comes out in seven colours: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet. Rainbows do not stay long — we enjoy them, then we draw them so they stay with us."',
  notice:'The rainbow card follows a scientist with round glasses and a white lab coat: she points up at a big rainbow arcing over a house and trees, kneels by a puddle holding a special glass that bends light, presents a whiteboard with seven labelled colour dots — Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Indigo, Violet — shades her eyes from a bright sun, colours a rainbow band picture with crayons, and gestures between a rain-cloud bubble and a smiling-sun bubble as if explaining how both make it together.',
  ask:'"What two things make a rainbow?" "Which colour comes first? Which is last?" "Why do rainbows go away?" "What could we do to keep the memory?"',
  childSay:'Your child might chant the seven colours unprompted for days, announce "Sun plus rain makes rainbow!" from the back seat of the car, or declare the mist-spray rainbow the best discovery of the summer.',
  real:'Keep rainbow-spotting alive all season: after every shower, check the sky in the shower’s direction with the sun at your back. Garden sprinklers, car washes and fountain spray all hide little rainbows on sunny days.',
  play:'Rainbow restaurant: lay out coloured paper "plates" and serve toy food sorted to each colour band. Rainbow hunt at home: find one object per colour, laid on the floor in arc order. Then the big art: a seven-band crayon rainbow, one colour chanted per band.',
  tips:'When a real rainbow fades mid-hug, let the disappointment exist and answer it with action: "Let us draw it quick, before we forget!" The drawing ritual converts loss into making. And learn the colours yourself by heart — confidence is contagious.',
  check:'Ask: "What makes a rainbow? Name the colours you remember. Why can we not keep a rainbow?" Sun-plus-rain, any ordered run of the seven, and a soft "it fades" — that is the full arc.',
  safety:'Rainbow-hunting means back to the sun — say it out loud together: we look at the rainbow sky, never at the sun. Wet grass and slopes are slippery, so rainbow-chasing feet walk. Spray-bottle rainbows are for sunny days, never for eyes.'},
 'wind':{
  title:'Wind',one:'Air you cannot see but can always catch — feeling, watching and riding the invisible push.',
  seoTitle:'Wind Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Moving Air with Socks, Leaves and Kites (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what wind is, how to feel and see moving air, wind sock and leaf clues, hat-holding and safe kite-flying practice.',
  intro:'This lesson makes invisible air visible: your child learns what wind is, gathers the clues that show it blowing, and ends with the season’s best prize — flying a kite safely in an open field.',
  what:'Wind is air that is moving. Air is everywhere around us, and when a lot of it travels in one direction we feel it as a breeze on our cheeks, hear it in the trees and see it in everything it pushes.',
  mean:'Wind has jobs and moods. Gentle wind dries washing, cools hot picnics, carries seeds and spins the big turbines that make electricity. Strong wind bends trees, waves washing on the line and chases hats down the street.',
  know:'Your child needs three wind ideas: wind is moving air we feel but never see; we know wind is blowing because of what it moves — leaves, branches, washing, wind socks; and windy days ask for hat-holding, and give kite-flying.',
  why:'Wind is the purest inference lesson in early science: the cause is invisible, so the child reasons entirely from effects. That clue-gathering — leaves moving, sock pointing, hair flying — is the exact thinking scientists use to study things too big or small to see.',
  dev:'Inference from evidence, gradient words (breeze, gust, gale), cause-and-effect through kite flying, gross-motor play against resistance, and safety judgement about flying objects and open spaces.',
  vocab:'wind, breeze, gust, blow, wind sock, kite, flutter, gusty',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Wind is air that is moving — we cannot see air, but we can see what it does!"\n2) Read the picture: the engineer in a white hard hat and high-visibility vest feels the breeze on her hand, watches a red-and-white wind sock stretch out, sees leaves blown off a tree, holds her hat on her head, flies a red-and-yellow kite and points at a tablet that says WINDY. Behind her: giant white wind turbines.\n3) Collect wind clues right now: hold a hand out the door — feel it? Throw a scrap of tissue — watch it fly. Which way is the wind pointing today?\n4) Sort the moods: a breeze tickles, a gust pushes, a gale grabs hats. Act all three with your hands and voices.\n5) Book the kite: windy day + open field + grown-up = kite day. Practise the run-and-release at home first with a ribbon on a stick.',
  say:'"Wind is air on the move. We cannot see it, but we can see what it does: the leaves dance, the washing flaps, the wind sock stretches out. On windy days we hold our hats — and on a good steady windy day, we fly a kite!"',
  notice:'On this card a wind-farm engineer wears a white hard hat, a navy shirt and a yellow high-visibility vest with a wind-turbine badge: she feels the wind on her raised palm, stands beside a striped wind sock on a pole, watches green leaves blow from a branch, steadies her hard hat with both hands, flies a red-and-yellow diamond kite with bows on its tail, and shows a tablet with wind icons and the word WINDY. Tall white turbines turn in the background.',
  ask:'"What is wind made of?" "How do we know the wind is blowing if we cannot see it?" "What does the wind do to our hats?" "Where do kites fly best?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The wind is pushing the leaves!" "A breeze is small and a gale is HUGE," or the field report: "My kite flew because the wind pulled it up."',
  real:'Count wind workers on any street: washing lines, flags, wind turbines on hills, the trees leaning after a storm. Weather-vane visits, harbour sails and windy-day bus stops all show the air doing visible work.',
  play:'Wind orchestra: paper strips, balloons and a fan — which things does the fan move, which ignore it? Then streamer racing: whose streamer flies straightest? Finish with the classic — faces into the breeze, eyes closed, one deep breath: you just felt the whole sky move.',
  tips:'Answer "what makes wind?" simply and honestly: air moves from place to place, like a crowd shuffling — warm air rises, cool air slides in, and that shuffle is wind. No need for pressure maps at four; the shuffle picture is enough and it is true.',
  check:'Ask: "What is wind? Name two things the wind moves. What do we hold on a windy day?" Moving air, any two honest movers, and hats — the invisible is now visible.',
  safety:'Skip tree-lined streets and building sites on gusty days — branches and debris travel. Kites fly in open fields far from roads and power lines, launch and landing by a grown-up. And secure or leave home anything the wind can turn into a projectile: hats, balloons, lightweight toys.'}
,
 'snow':{
  title:'Snow',one:'Frozen flakes that fall soft and slow — catching, measuring and playing in the sky’s white confetti.',
  seoTitle:'Snow Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Ice-Crystal Flakes and Snow-Day Play (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what snow is and why flakes have star shapes, catching and measuring snow, the melt experiment and warm-kit rules for snow days.',
  intro:'This lesson explains what snow actually is — frozen water falling in tiny crystals — and turns the first snowfall into a full science morning: catch, measure, melt, then warm up properly.',
  what:'Snow is water that froze into tiny ice crystals high in the clouds and fell as soft white flakes. Each flake is a little six-sided star of ice, and each one melts back into a drop of water when it lands on something warm.',
  mean:'Snow needs cold all the way down — the whole air column must be chilly or the flakes arrive as rain or sleet. Fresh snow covers the ground in a white blanket, muffles sounds, records every footprint and melts back into water that feeds the spring.',
  know:'Your child needs three snow ideas: snow is frozen water in crystal flakes; flakes melt on warm hands because our bodies are warm; and snow play asks for the full warm kit — coat, hat, gloves and boots — with warm-up breaks inside.',
  why:'Snow is the most touchable science in childhood: children hold crystals, watch them vanish, pack them, stack them and measure them. Melting is their first change-of-state experiment, and the ruler in the snow is their first measuring instrument used for a real question.',
  dev:'Change-of-state understanding (ice ⇄ water), measurement skills with real stakes, cold-body awareness and self-advocacy ("my gloves are wet"), shape observation on sleeves, and the sequencing of a full dressing routine.',
  vocab:'snow, snowflake, melt, freeze, ice, frost, snowball, slippery',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Snow is rain that froze into tiny ice stars on the way down."\n2) Read the picture: the girl in a blue pom-pom hat catches a falling snowflake on her hand, kneels to measure the snow with a big ruler, fastens her warm coat, rolls a snowball, walks carefully on an icy path and reads a forecast that says Snow, minus 2 degrees.\n3) Teach the melt: catch one flake on a dark sleeve and watch it closely; then one on a warm palm — gone! Our hands are warm enough to turn snow back into water.\n4) Measure together: stand a ruler in the snow, read the number, check again after an hour. Snow grows and shrinks — like a science pet.\n5) Drill the kit in order: coat, hat, gloves, boots — then the penguin walk on icy paths, tiny steps, knees soft.',
  say:'"Snow is frozen water falling in tiny star-shaped flakes. Catch one on your sleeve and look quick — then watch it melt on your hand, because our hands are warm. In the snow we wear all our warm kit and walk like penguins on the slippery bits."',
  notice:'On this card a girl in a blue pom-pom beanie with a snowflake pattern and a blue winter jacket: holds her hand out to catch one big six-pointed snowflake, kneels beside a numbered ruler standing in the snow to measure its depth, zips her coat up to her chin, rolls a big round snowball, walks with careful steps along a snowy path between fences and snowy pines, and points at a tablet reading "Snow −2°C" with little flakes on the screen.',
  ask:'"What is snow made of?" "Why did the flake melt on your hand?" "How deep is the snow on our ruler?" "What do we wear before snow play?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Snowflakes are little ice stars!" "It melted because my hand is warm," or the kit chant: "Coat, hat, gloves, boots — ready for snow!"',
  real:'Watch snow line rooftops, hide the grass and outline every branch after a night of steady fall. Find where snow melts first (sunny edges, warm car roofs) and where it lingers (shady corners). Track a snowman through its slow, sagging goodbye.',
  play:'Snow-kitchen: bowls, spoons and cupcake trays turn fresh snow into the year’s best sensory table. Melt-race: one cup of snow indoors, one outside — which melts faster, and why? Then the snowball school: pack, roll, stop — and the family rule about where snowballs may never fly.',
  tips:'Keep spare gloves drying by the radiator — the wet-glove swap keeps the outing alive twice as long. And capture the melting question every time: "Where did the snow go?" is a genuine science prompt, and "it turned to water!" is a genuinely correct answer.',
  check:'Ask: "What is snow? Why do flakes melt in your hand? Name your snow-day kit." Frozen water, warm hands, and any full run of coat-hat-gloves-boots — the lesson has landed.',
  safety:'Wet plus cold chills bodies fast — full kit with waterproof boots, and dry gloves the moment they soak through. Penguin steps on icy paths, never eat snow, snowballs never at faces or cars, and inside when shivers start. Snow that has been played in is for playing, not for tasting.'},
 'weather-forecast':{
  title:'Weather Forecast',one:'The sky’s plan, told in advance — how forecasts help families choose clothes and stay safe.',
  seoTitle:'Weather Forecast Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Sky Plans and Family Planning (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a weather forecast is, reading sun-rain-cloud icons, a weather-reporter role play and using forecasts to plan the day.',
  intro:'This lesson turns the morning forecast into a family ritual: what forecasts are, who makes them, how to read the little icons, and how the sky’s plan decides the coat, the hat and the day.',
  what:'A weather forecast is a careful prediction of what the sky will do next — today, tomorrow, all week. Weather scientists read maps, clouds and instruments, then share the plan on TV, radio and phones.',
  mean:'Forecasts turn unknown skies into known plans: rain icon means the wellies come out, sun icon means the sun hat, storm warning means the family stays close to home. The forecast does not control the sky — it just reads the clues early.',
  know:'Your child needs three forecast ideas: the forecast tells us what the sky plans before it happens; sun, cloud and rain icons are the forecast’s picture words; and families use the forecast to choose clothes and to stay safe when big weather comes.',
  why:'Checking the forecast is daily evidence-based decision-making in child-size form: information → choice → action → check the result. Children adore grading the forecaster’s homework — and grading an adult’s work respectfully is a genuine critical-thinking exercise.',
  dev:'Prediction and verification, icon literacy, planning and sequencing (what comes first — coat or boots?), weather vocabulary across days (today, tomorrow, the whole week), and the family-habit feeling that preparation beats surprise.',
  vocab:'forecast, predict, weather reporter, plan, icon, tomorrow, weekly, storm warning',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "A weather forecast is the sky’s plan, told to us before it happens."\n2) Read the picture: the presenter in a yellow blazer points at a big weather map, a board with two thermometers reading 18 degrees, a rain-cloud board, a rail with a yellow raincoat, a blue jacket and a purple jumper, a "Tomorrow" board with a sun behind a cloud, and a five-day chart from Monday to Friday with sun and cloud icons.\n3) Play icon-reading: show today’s forecast on your phone — sun, cloud or rain? Ask your child to "read" it back to you like a story.\n4) Let the forecast dress you both: rain icon → raincoat and boots; sun icon → sun hat. Make the choice out loud so the reasoning is heard.\n5) crown the family weather reporter: once a week, your child presents the weather — paper-tube microphone, big gestures, the works.',
  say:'"The weather reporter looks at the sky’s clues and tells us the plan: rain or sun, warm or cold, windy or calm. We listen to the forecast, then we get ready — the right clothes, the right bag, the right day."',
  notice:'On this card a TV weather presenter in a yellow blazer stands in a studio: she presents a huge weather map with clouds and coloured weather lines, points at a board with two thermometers reading 18°C, shows a board with a big rain cloud and raindrops, stands beside a clothes rail holding a yellow rain jacket, a blue jacket and a purple jumper, points to a "Tomorrow" card showing a sun behind a cloud, and presents a weekly chart — Monday sunny, Tuesday rain, Wednesday cloud, Thursday partly sunny, Friday sunny — with a temperature number under each day.',
  ask:'"What does the weather reporter tell us?" "What do we take outside if the forecast says rain?" "What does the sun icon mean?" "Did yesterday’s forecast get it right?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The forecast says rain — boots please!" or deliver a full bulletin: "Today is cloudy, tomorrow is sunny — that is the weather, thank you!"',
  real:'Make the morning forecast check a fixed ritual — while shoes go on, the sky’s plan gets read. Let your child spot forecast icons on phones, roadside signs, newspapers and TV. After big-weather days, hold the verdict together: right sky, wrong sky, half right?',
  play:'Weather studio: a cardboard TV frame, a paper-tube microphone and yesterday’s drawings as props — your child presents today, tomorrow and "the weekend", complete with sound effects. Then the forecast-vs-reality chart: two columns, sticker for every correct call.',
  tips:'Never pretend forecasts are perfect — the misses are teachable gold: "The sky changed its mind; even weather scientists keep learning." And keep the ritual tiny: one icon, one clothing choice, ten seconds. Rituals that survive are rituals that stay small.',
  check:'Ask: "Who tells us the sky’s plan? What do we grab when the forecast says rain? What do we do when a big storm is forecast?" Reporter, raincoat, stay home together — planning is now a family skill.',
  safety:'Teach the storm-warning response explicitly: when grown-ups say a big storm is coming, we stay indoors, away from windows, and let the wild sky pass. Children can help — fetching the torch, counting the batteries — because helping prepares, and preparing calms.'},
 'sunny-day':{
  title:'Sunny Day',one:'A wide-blue, shadow-sharp day — how to wring the joy out of sunshine and stay cool doing it.',
  seoTitle:'Sunny Day Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Shadow Play and the Hat-Water-Shade Routine (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what makes a day sunny, shadow tracking and shade hunts, the midday-sun routine and full sunny-day play planning.',
  intro:'This lesson defines the sunny day your child already loves, then upgrades it: shadows to chase, shade hunts to run, and the hat-water-shade routine that keeps the whole day comfortable from breakfast to bath.',
  what:'A sunny day is a day when the sun lights the sky almost without clouds: blue above, bright light below, sharp shadows everywhere, and warm ground underfoot from mid-morning on.',
  mean:'Sunny days have a rhythm. Morning sun is gentle and shadows are long; midday sun is strong and shadows shrink; evening sun cools and shadows stretch again. Families who read that rhythm play outside in the easy hours and rest in shade at the peak.',
  know:'Your child needs three sunny-day ideas: sun makes light, warmth and shadows; the midday sun is the strongest, so shade and hats matter most then; and a sunny-day kit — hat, water bottle, sunscreen from a grown-up — goes with us every time.',
  why:'The sunny day is the strongest cause-and-effect classroom outdoors: stand in light, warm; step into shade, cool; jump, and the shadow jumps. Children who plan around the sun learn that routines are tools, not rules — and they own those tools by age five.',
  dev:'Time-of-day concepts tied to felt experience, spatial reasoning through shadow play, planning and self-care routines, heat awareness, and the pure gross-motor joy of a full outdoor day spent well.',
  vocab:'sunny, shadow, shade, midday, bright, sun hat, water bottle, cool',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "A sunny day is when the sun lights up everything — the sky, the ground, our shadows."\n2) Read the picture: the park ranger shades her eyes to check the sky, adjusts her wide hat, drinks from a water bottle, plays resting in the shade of a big tree, squeezes sunscreen onto her hand and points at a forecast board showing three days — sunny, partly cloudy, cloudy.\n3) Do the shadow check right now: jump — did your shadow jump? Stand still — did it freeze? The sun is the puppeteer.\n4) Run a shade hunt: find three cool resting spots in the garden or park, and name which one you would pick at midday.\n5) Build the routine into a chant: hat on, bottle filled, sunscreen by a grown-up — then out to play. Chant it at the door, every sunny morning.',
  say:'"A sunny day means the sun is lighting everything up. We wear our hats, we drink water, we play in the shade when the sun is at its strongest — and our shadows play with us all day long."',
  notice:'On this card a park ranger in a khaki hat and uniform: shades her eyes with one hand beneath a bright sun, adjusts her hat brim under a tree, drinks from a blue bottle, rests happily against a big shady trunk, squeezes sunscreen from a bottle with a yellow cap onto her arm, and points at a "Today’s Forecast" board with three columns — a sunny 82, a partly cloudy 76 and a cloudy 70.',
  ask:'"What does the sun do on a sunny day?" "When are our shadows longest — morning or midday?" "Where is the best shade spot?" "What goes in our sunny-day kit?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "My shadow is longer than me now!" "Midday shade time — then back out to play," or recite the door chant unprompted: "Hat, bottle, sunscreen!"',
  real:'Track the family shadow on the walk to school across a whole week. Notice who else uses shade: dogs under benches, market stalls under canopies, cafés with umbrellas. Check the forecast board or app together and rate tomorrow’s sunny chances.',
  play:'Shadow circus: make your shadow into a rabbit, a giant, a crab — hands and feet tell the story. Shadow hopscotch: jump only on shady patches. Sundial snack: mark a biscuit-tin shadow with a stone at breakfast, check it again at lunch — the sun moved the shadow, and the snack tastes better outside anyway.',
  tips:'Anchor the routine to the felt difference, not the lecture: one moment hatless in the sun, then hat on — "cooler, right?" The body remembers what the mouth explains. And never skip your own hat; the routine holds only if grown-ups visibly obey it too.',
  check:'Ask: "What three things does a sunny day give us?" (Light, warmth, shadows.) "What do we do at midday?" (Shade and hats.) "What is in our sunny-day kit?" — Three routines named, one lesson landed.',
  safety:'The midday sun is the strongest — shade rests and plenty of water in the peak hours, sunscreen applied by a grown-up before the day starts, never a stare upward, and never anyone left in a parked car, not even for one minute.'},
 'cloudy-day':{
  title:'Cloudy Day',one:'A soft-grey, softly-lit day — the gardener’s favourite weather and the sky’s calmest mood.',
  seoTitle:'Cloudy Day Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Soft Light and the Garden’s Grey-Day Jobs (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a cloudy day feels like, counting cloud cover, grey-day garden jobs and why soft light changes shadows.',
  intro:'This lesson gives the cloudy day its due: what the grey ceiling really is, how the light and shadows change, and the perfectly-cool outdoor jobs — planting, watering, walking — that grey days are secretly best for.',
  what:'A cloudy day is when clouds cover most or all of the sky. The light turns soft and silvery, shadows fade to faint smudges, and the air often feels cooler because the sunshine is filtered through the cloud blankets.',
  mean:'Cloudy does not automatically mean rainy — clouds have stages: a few white visitors, a half-grey sky, full grey cover, and the heavy dark kind that drips. Reading which stage the sky is in decides the day: coat, no coat, or umbrella in the bag.',
  know:'Your child needs three cloudy-day ideas: clouds filter the sun and soften the light; cloudy is not always rainy — we check which grey it is; and cloudy days are cool-work days, perfect for gardens and long walks.',
  why:'The cloudy day teaches nuance — the sky has more than two settings. Grading the cloud cover (a bit, a lot, all of it) builds observation on a scale, and the reframe "grey days are garden days" widens a child’s idea of which days are good days.',
  dev:'Observation on a gradient, comparison vocabulary, the shadow experiment in reverse (soft light, faint shadows), care-of-plants routines, and emotional reframe practice — finding the good in a quietly grey day.',
  vocab:'cloudy, grey, soft light, cover, drizzle, garden, mild, gloomy',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "A cloudy day is when the clouds pull their blankets over the whole sky."\n2) Read the picture: the gardener in a yellow hat and green jacket stands with her spade under big grey-white clouds while the circles show her looking at clouds, watering plants, zipping a light jacket, holding a thermometer, planting seeds and talking about rain with a little rain cloud in her thought bubble.\n3) Grade today’s sky together: a few clouds, half covered, or all grey? Give the sky a cloud score from one to ten.\n4) Do the shadow check: on full cloud, shadows go faint — the sun’s light is arriving gently, from everywhere at once.\n5) Choose one grey-day job: watering the plants, planting seeds or a long cool walk — then go and do it, wellies standing by in case.',
  say:'"The clouds have covered the sky like a big grey blanket. The sun is still up there, resting behind them. Cloudy days are cool and soft — the garden loves them, and so do gardeners. Let us check: is today a cloudy day or a rainy day?"',
  notice:'On this card a gardener in a wide yellow hat, green jacket and green boots stands holding a spade in a garden with raised beds and a wooden fence, under a sky full of grey-white clouds. Around her in circles: she looks up at the clouds, waters flowers with a blue watering can, zips her light jacket, holds up a thermometer, plants seeds in a wooden tray, and talks about rain — a small rain cloud floats in her thought bubble.',
  ask:'"What do the clouds do to the sunshine today?" "Is a cloudy day always a rainy day?" "What happened to our shadows?" "What jobs are perfect for a grey day?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The sky has its grey blanket on!" "Shadows are hiding today," or the gardener’s report: "I watered the whole flower bed and I was not even hot."',
  real:'Compare feelings across weathers: what does the street look like on a cloudy day versus a sunny one? Notice the cool-work crowd — gardeners, dog walkers, builders — who get on with it happily under grey. Keep the daily cloud score going for a week.',
  play:'Grey-day picnic: the same blanket, the same sandwiches, just softer light — normalise outdoor joy under cloud. Play shadow-hide-and-seek: on a cloudy day nobody can see the seekers’ shadows, a genuinely different game. Then plant something — grey and cool is prime planting weather.',
  tips:'Watch your own weather talk: children hear everything, and "grey old day" teaches grey means gloomy. Try "soft day" or "good garden weather" and let the reframe do quiet work. And keep the jacket rule honest — cloudy air is often cooler than it looks.',
  check:'Ask: "What do clouds do to the light? Does cloudy always mean rainy? Name one perfect cloudy-day job." Soft light, not always, and any honest garden answer — the grey day is now a good day.',
  safety:'Grey skies still let sun through — for long midday outings, the hat stays in the bag and on the head. And check whether the grey is heading for rain: a heavy, darkening sky means the coat — maybe the umbrella — comes along too.'},
 'rainy-day':{
  title:'Rainy Day',one:'A boots-and-puddles day — getting ready in the right order and finding the fun in a soaky world.',
  seoTitle:'Rainy Day Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Get-Ready Sequence and Puddle Joy (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a rainy day brings, the coat-boots-umbrella sequence, keeping things dry, a rain-measuring bowl and polite puddle rules.',
  intro:'This lesson owns the rainy day end to end: what the sky is doing, the get-ready sequence that keeps everyone dry, the postal worker’s trick for protecting precious things, and the puddle etiquette that makes splashing a joy.',
  what:'A rainy day is when the clouds drop their water steadily over the neighbourhood: grey skies, dripping trees, growing puddles, shiny roads and the whole world smelling of wet stone and green things.',
  mean:'Rainy days are preparation days — the families who get ready in the right order stay dry and cheerful, and the rain becomes a background drum instead of a day-ruiner. Rain also has jobs: it waters everything, washes the streets and refills the puddle supply.',
  know:'Your child needs three rainy-day ideas: rainy days ask for the gear sequence — raincoat, boots, umbrella; precious things stay dry in bags and plastic sleeves; and puddles are for polite splashing — small ones yes, road-side ones never.',
  why:'The rainy day is a sequencing gym: coat before boots or boots before coat? Zip before hood or hood before zip? Every wet morning rehearses ordering, and the payoff is immediate — the child who dresses right stays warm, dry and proud all the way to the door.',
  dev:'Sequencing and self-dressing mastery, responsibility for belongings (the dry letter!), sensory vocabulary for rain sounds, judgement calls about puddles and roads, and the emotional shift from "rain ruins play" to "rain changes play".',
  vocab:'rainy, umbrella, raincoat, boots, puddle, splash, drip, soak',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "A rainy day is the clouds watering the whole town — and we have our rain plan."\n2) Read the picture: the postal worker walks under a big navy umbrella past wet houses; the small panels show him opening the umbrella, zipping a yellow raincoat, pulling on yellow boots, pointing at a puddle, sliding a letter into a clear plastic sleeve to keep it dry, and checking a phone forecast that reads 12 degrees.\n3) Build the sequence together: coat first, then boots, then umbrella last — because the umbrella needs a hand for the door.\n4) Teach the keep-things-dry trick: precious things ride in bags or plastic sleeves, just like the letter on the card. What would YOUR child protect from the rain?\n5) Set the puddle rules with a smile: splash the small garden puddles, step around the kerb-side ones, and never — never — splash towards a road.',
  say:'"It is a rainy day, so we follow the rain plan: raincoat zipped, boots on, umbrella up. Our precious things ride in a dry bag. We walk carefully near roads, and the small puddles in the garden are all ours to splash."',
  notice:'On this card a postal worker in a navy cap and light-blue shirt carries a navy mail bag: the big picture shows him walking under a wide navy umbrella on a wet street with houses and a white picket fence; the smaller panels show him opening the folded umbrella, zipping a bright yellow raincoat, pulling on yellow rain boots, crouching to point at a puddle, sliding a letter into a clear plastic sleeve, and holding a phone showing a rain-cloud forecast at 12°C.',
  ask:'"What comes first — the coat or the umbrella?" "How do we keep precious things dry?" "Which puddles are for splashing?" "What is the rain doing for the garden today?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Coat, boots, umbrella — in that order!" "My letter goes in the dry bag," or the puddle verdict: "That one is a splasher, that one is a monster — we step around it."',
  real:'Watch the rain workers: postal carriers, delivery riders, road crews — all dressed for it, all still smiling. Notice what the rain feeds on the walk: thirsty flower beds, full gutters singing, the birdbath brimming. Start a rain bowl on the windowsill — outside, lid off — and measure the night’s catch.',
  play:'Rain-band: wooden spoons on pots, rice in a bottle, fingers on windows — which instrument sounds most like the rain outside? Dry-boat regatta: paper boats race along a gutter’s edge (grown-up pace, shallow water). And the classic rainy-day shelter: a blanket fort with a book and a bowl of snacks while the real rain drums on the roof.',
  tips:'Keep the gear at child height by the door — independence dies on a high hook. Let your child own one piece completely (the umbrella choice, the boot colour). And join the splash: one dignified grown-up stomp in a garden puddle buys a year of rainy-day cooperation.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the rain plan? How do we keep special things dry? Which puddles may we splash?" Coat-boots-umbrella, bags-and-sleeves, garden-only — the rainy day is now an owned day.',
  safety:'Wet roads make cars slower to stop — hands held at crossings, walking feet on shiny pavements, never any splashing towards the road. Watch for slippery leaves and painted lines, keep umbrella tips away from eyes, and come home to dry clothes before the shivers start.'}
,
 'stormy-day':{
  title:'Stormy Day',one:'Lightning, thunder and the cosy indoors — turning the sky’s loudest show into a counted, calmed event.',
  seoTitle:'Stormy Day Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Thunder Counting and Indoors Storm Safety (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what storms are, the flash-then-boom order and thunder counting, the storm-safety poster rules and cosy indoor storm plans.',
  intro:'This lesson takes the fear out of the sky’s loudest weather: what lightning and thunder really are, why the boom follows the flash, and the three storm rules from the card’s own poster — plus a thunder-counting game that makes waiting a sport.',
  what:'A stormy day brings dark towering clouds, heavy rain, strong wind, flashes of lightning and rumbles of thunder, all together. It is the sky at its most dramatic — and it usually passes quickly.',
  mean:'Lightning is a giant spark of electricity jumping inside or between clouds. Thunder is the sound of that spark super-heating the air. Light travels faster than sound, so we always see the flash first and hear the boom afterwards.',
  know:'Your child needs three storm ideas: lightning is a giant sky spark and thunder is its sound; the flash comes first and the boom chases it; and our storm plan is the poster’s three rules — stay inside, stay away from trees, stay away from plugs and wires.',
  why:'Storm fear is among the most common childhood fears — and among the most reducible. Naming the order (flash, count, boom) converts a mystery into a countable game; the poster’s rules give the body a script; and a cosy den gives the feelings somewhere warm to sit.',
  dev:'Sequencing (flash → count → boom), counting under mild excitement, fear-processing through play and explanation, family-safety scripting, and the deep reassurance of routines that hold even when the sky does not.',
  vocab:'storm, lightning, thunder, spark, rumble, inside, safe, cosy',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "A storm is a big cloud having a very loud day — and we watch it from our safe, cosy inside."\n2) Read the picture: the scientist watches a lightning flash from her window, listens for thunder with a hand to her ear, reads in an armchair by a lamp, closes the window latch, checks a storm forecast on a tablet and points at a storm-safety poster that says: stay inside, stay away from trees, stay away from plugs and wires.\n3) Teach the order with hands: flash (fingers flick), count (one-Mississippi, two-Mississippi…), BOOM (stamp). The longer the count, the farther away the storm.\n4) Walk the three rules as a little march: inside! away from trees! away from plugs! Then draw them as a family poster.\n5) Build the storm plan: when the forecast says storm, we shut windows, bring in the cushions and make the den — the storm is the TV tonight.',
  say:'"A storm makes big flashes and loud rumbles. The flash is lightning — a giant spark — and the boom is thunder, chasing right after it because sound runs slower than light. We stay inside, away from windows, and watch the show together from our cosy spot."',
  notice:'This stormy card keeps a scientist with glasses and a white lab coat indoors: she watches a fork of lightning over dark rooftops through the window, cups her hand behind her ear to catch the thunder, reads a blue book in an armchair beside a glowing lamp, turns the window latch shut against the rain, points at a tablet with a storm-cloud forecast, and presents a "Storm Safety" poster with three picture rules — a house for stay inside, a crossed-out tree for stay away from trees, and a crossed-out plug for stay away from plugs and wires.',
  ask:'"Which comes first — the flash or the boom?" "What is thunder the sound of?" "Name the three storm rules." "What is our family’s cosy storm spot?"',
  childSay:'Your child might chant the rules as a march: "Inside! No trees! No plugs!" count the flash-to-boom gap proudly, or declare mid-storm: "The storm is far — the count was BIG."',
  real:'Watch how the neighbourhood answers storms: windows closing, market stalls packing up, buses easing along, then everyone back out after. Point to lightning rods and safe buildings — grown-ups have been outsmarting storms for centuries, and the house is on our side.',
  play:'Thunder theatre: flash the lights off, count together, then drum the sofa for thunder — count loud storms in real time from the den. Storm-den night: cushions, torch, picture books about weather, cocoa. The goal is positive association: storms end in dens, cocoa and stories, every single time.',
  tips:'Your calm is the lesson — narrate your own steadiness: "There is the flash… here comes the count… boom!" If your child is storm-shy, let them control the exposure: den flap open or shut, torch on or off, watching or just listening. Control is the antidote to fear, and cocoa helps.',
  check:'Ask: "What is lightning? Why do we hear thunder after the flash? What are the storm rules?" A sky spark, sound runs slower, and the three-rule march — fear has officially become knowledge.',
  safety:'Storm rules for small people, from the card itself: stay inside, away from windows; stay away from trees — never shelter under one; stay away from plugs and wires during the loudest part. If outside when a storm starts, come in at once — the sky gets the whole stage.'},
 'windy-day':{
  title:'Windy Day',one:'A hat-grabbing, kite-lifting day — reading the strong wind and riding it safely.',
  seoTitle:'Windy Day Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Kite Flying and Windy-Day Safety (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what makes a windy day different from a breeze, securing hats and windows, kite-flying steps and the flying-object check.',
  intro:'This lesson takes the breeze your child already knows and turns the dial to windy: how a windy day differs, what the wind moves and shakes, the hat-and-window preparations, and the step-by-step joy of flying a kite in an open field.',
  what:'A windy day is when the air moves strongly enough to grab attention: hair streaming sideways, trees swaying, washing snapping, leaves skittering and windows humming. Windy is the breeze’s big sister — same air, more push.',
  mean:'Strong wind has real power: it bends whole trees, blows hats into next week and rattles doors. It also does glorious things — lifts kites, flies flags, races clouds. The windy-day skill is respect plus enjoyment: secure the loose things, then go feel the force.',
  know:'Your child needs three windy-day ideas: windy means the air pushes hard enough to move lots of things; we secure hats, windows and light objects before playing; and windy days are kite days — in open spaces, away from roads and wires.',
  why:'A windy day is felt physics: your child leans into the push, watches leaves fly and feels a kite tug the string. Cause and effect become whole-body — run and the kite climbs, stop and it dips — and the safety habits (secured hats, shut windows) are rehearsals for real storm readiness.',
  dev:'Physical force awareness, preparation routines (hat straps, window checks), kite-handling coordination, observational sorting (what moves fast, what barely sways), and the confidence of playing competently in big weather.',
  vocab:'windy, gust, kite, secure, flutter, sway, blow, open field',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "A windy day is when the wind pushes hard enough to move everything — even us!"\n2) Read the picture: the kite instructor in a blue cap stands on a grassy field flying a blue-and-yellow kite on a long string; the circles show her feeling the wind on her face, holding the string with both hands, watching leaves fly past, pressing her cap on with both hands, closing a window latch, and pointing at a forecast that reads 18 degrees with a windy cloud icon.\n3) Do the loose-things check before any windy outing: hats on with straps, balloons held tight, windows latched at home. What else could the wind take?\n4) Feel the force game: lean forward into the wind with your whole body — the wind is pushing back! Take three steps against it, then let it push you home, giggling.\n5) Fly the kite in order: open field, grown-up launches, child runs holding the string — run and it climbs, slow down and it dips. That tug in the string is the wind, saying hello.',
  say:'"On a windy day the air pushes hard — it moves leaves, hats and even kites! We strap our hats on, we shut our windows, and then we go out and feel the push. And in a big open field, the wind will lift a kite right up into the sky."',
  notice:'On this card a kite instructor in a light-blue jacket and blue cap flies a blue-and-yellow diamond kite on a white string in the middle of a grassy field. The labelled circles show her: feeling the wind with eyes closed and palm up, holding the kite string with both hands, watching two green leaves blow through the air, pressing her cap down with both hands, closing a window latch, and pointing at a "TODAY" forecast card with a windy cloud icon reading 18°C.',
  ask:'"What is the wind moving today?" "What do we check before a windy walk?" "What happens to the kite when you run? When you stop?" "Where do kites fly — and where never?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The wind pushed me home!" "My hat is strapped, my window is shut," or the kite report: "I ran and the wind pulled the string — I could FEEL it!"',
  real:'Watch the wind workers after a windy night: flags worn out, bins moved, branches down, kites patched. Notice which things sway safely (trees, lamps) and which things should never sway (cranes, wobbly fences). Secure-the-garden becomes a windy-day ritual: balls in, cushions in, trampoline tied.',
  play:'Wind gauge: hold out different papers — tissue, napkin, card — and rank which the wind moves fastest. Sock-in-the-air relay. Then the main event, the kite: practise the run indoors with a ribbon, then take the real kite to the biggest open space you can find. The tug on that string is the whole lesson in one feeling.',
  tips:'Do the preparations as a duo, out loud, every windy time: "Hats? Strapped. Windows? Shut. Loose toys? In the box." The chant becomes the child’s own pre-wind checklist. And manage the down draft: windy-day tiredness is real — big air days end with big snacks and early baths.',
  check:'Ask: "What kind of day is it when the wind pushes hard? What do we strap on? Where do kites fly?" Windy, hats, open fields far from wires — your child now reads big wind like a local.',
  safety:'Windy-day rules: strap the hat, latch the windows, bring in the light things. Walk clear of old trees, scaffolding and building sites where the wind shakes things loose. Kites fly in open fields only — never near roads, power lines or cables — and the launch is always a grown-up job.'},
 'snowy-day':{
  title:'Snowy Day',one:'The white, quiet day — full warm kit, penguin steps, snowplough watching and one perfect snow angel.',
  seoTitle:'Snowy Day Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Snow-Day Routines and Snowplough Watching (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a snowy day looks and sounds like, the full dressing order, penguin walking, snowplough safety and the snow-angel finale.',
  intro:'This lesson choreographs the snowy day from first look out the window to the cocoa return: what the white world is doing, the dressing order that keeps it fun, the penguin walk for icy patches, and the jobs — snowploughs! — that keep the town moving.',
  what:'A snowy day is when snow falls and settles: white roofs, white paths, footprints telling who went where, and a special hush because fresh snow softens every sound in the street.',
  mean:'Snowy days change how everything works: walking slows, cars crawl or stop, schools sometimes close, and big machines come out to help — snowploughs push the snow aside so buses and ambulances can get through.',
  know:'Your child needs three snowy-day ideas: snowy days ask for the full kit in order — coat, hat, gloves, boots; icy paths need penguin steps, tiny and careful; and we never walk or play near a working snowplough — we watch from far away with a grown-up.',
  why:'A snowy day is the year’s biggest routine test: five garments, one order, cold consequences. Mastering it feels enormous to a four-year-old. Add the neighbourhood drama of the snowplough and the sheer joy of the first angel, and the day teaches preparation, patience and celebration at once.',
  dev:'Multi-step sequencing under excitement, balance and body control on slippery ground, community awareness (the plough keeps the town open), patience in dressing, and the pure sensory richness of the year’s most different-feeling day.',
  vocab:'snowy, snowplough, footprints, hush, icy, boots, gloves, snow angel',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "A snowy day turns the whole street white and quiet — and we have our snow plan."\n2) Read the picture: the snowplough driver in a yellow high-visibility coat zips it up, wraps a navy scarf, pulls on gloves, drives an orange snowplough with a big shovel pushing snow, lies in the snow making a snow angel, and points at a forecast reading minus 2 degrees.\n3) Drill the dressing order like a song: coat, then scarf, then hat, then gloves, boots last — boots last so nobody skates across the hallway.\n4) Practise penguin steps on the spot: tiny steps, feet flat, knees soft, arms out for balance. Now cross the "icy patch" (a rug) without a wobble.\n5) Make the angel: fall gently backward — a sit, then a soft plop — sweep arms up-down, legs in-out, stand up without stepping on the wings, and admire it from above like the picture.',
  say:'"A snowy day turns everything white and quiet. We dress in order — coat, scarf, hat, gloves, boots — then we walk like penguins on the slippery bits. We watch the snowplough from far away while it clears the road, and when the snow is fresh and soft, we make a snow angel."',
  notice:'On this card a snowplough driver with a beard, navy beanie and a yellow high-visibility winter coat: zips the coat to his chin, wraps a navy scarf around his neck, pulls on a navy glove, sits in the cab of an orange snowplough with amber lights pushing a mound of snow with its front shovel, lies spread-eagle in fresh snow next to a swept snow-angel shape, and points at a tablet showing snowflake icons and minus 2 degrees.',
  ask:'"What does a snowy day look and sound like?" "What is the dressing order?" "How do we walk on icy paths?" "What does the snowplough do — and how close do we go?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Coat, scarf, hat, gloves, boots — boots LAST!" "Penguin steps on the ice," or the angel review: "Mine has big wings — I did double arm sweeps!"',
  real:'Compare the street before and after the plough passes: where does the snow go? Which paths get gritted? Point out the real helpers — drivers, gritters, post — who keep the town running in the white. And count the bird tracks in fresh snow: the early birds signed their names.',
  play:'Footprint zoo: walk like different animals in fresh snow and guess each other’s prints. Snow-kitchen round two with muffin trays and spoons. The angel gallery: make a whole family of angels — big, small, and teddy-sized — then photograph the flock from a window before the next flurry files it all smooth.',
  tips:'Set the warm-up rhythm before the first mittened step: one outside block, one inside warm-up, repeat. Wet gloves go straight on the radiator, dry ones on. And savour the hush yourself — the child who hears you say "listen how quiet" learns that weather is also something to hear.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we put on first? How do we walk on ice? How close do we stand to a snowplough?" Coat-first, penguin steps, and very far away — the snowy day is fully choreographed.',
  safety:'Full kit every time — wet plus cold chills fast, and wet gloves get swapped, not endured. Penguin steps on ice, never running on shiny ground. Snowploughs are watched from far away with a grown-up, never followed or touched, and inside we go when the shivers start.'},
 'foggy-day':{
  title:'Foggy Day',one:'A cloud that came down to visit — seeing less, walking slower and staying brilliantly visible.',
  seoTitle:'Foggy Day Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Ground Clouds and Stay-Close Fog Rules (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what fog is, how it hides far things, the bright-vest and flashlight tricks, slow walking and the stay-with-a-grown-up rule.',
  intro:'This lesson explains the day the world goes milky: what fog is, how far it lets us see, why bright colours and lights matter, and the walking rules — slow steps, held hands, known paths — that make a foggy day an adventure instead of a worry.',
  what:'Fog is a cloud that forms at ground level — millions of tiny water drops floating in the air right where we walk. It scatters the light so that far-away things fade to grey ghosts and then vanish.',
  mean:'Fog changes how we use our senses: eyes lose their long range, so ears, memory and bright colours do more work. Everything familiar looks strange, distances are hard to judge, and everyone — walkers, drivers, cyclists — slows down and doubles their looking.',
  know:'Your child needs three fog ideas: fog is a cloud down at ground level that hides far things; in fog we make ourselves easy to see — bright coats, lights — and walk slowly; and the fog rule of rules: stay close to your grown-up, on paths you know.',
  why:'Fog is the rare weather that teaches sensory switching — from eyes to ears — plus the deep safety habit of visibility. A child who understands "I must be seen and stay close" in fog carries the same skill into dusk, car parks and crowded places for life.',
  dev:'Sensory adaptation (listening more, looking closer), distance judgement, colour-and-visibility reasoning, self-restraint at crossings, and the comfort of routine — same streets, same holds, slower pace, still safe.',
  vocab:'fog, mist, milky, visibility, bright, flashlight, slowly, vanish',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Fog is a cloud that came down to walk the streets with us."\n2) Read the picture: the crossing guard in a bright yellow high-visibility vest points to it proudly, shines a flashlight into the milky air, walks slowly across a zebra crossing, guides a small child in a green hat by the hand, holds up a stop sign while a car glows faintly behind, and checks a forecast reading 5 degrees.\n3) Play the disappearing game on the real fog: how far can you see before things fade? Count the steps to the nearest tree. Is it farther than yesterday?\n4) Do the bright test: stand five steps away. Which is easier to spot — the grey coat or the yellow one? THAT is why foggy-day coats are bright.\n5) Drill the fog walk: my hand, your hand; small steps; eyes AND ears working; cross only at the crossing where the guard helps.',
  say:'"Fog is a cloud that floats down low, right where we walk. It hides things that are far away, so we wear something bright, walk slowly, hold hands and use our ears as well as our eyes. The crossing guard helps us — in fog, everyone looks after everyone."',
  notice:'On this card a crossing guard in a navy cap and a bright yellow high-visibility vest: points at the words on the vest, shines a lit flashlight that glows in the milky air, walks slowly across a zebra crossing, holds the hand of a small child in a green bobble hat and teal coat, lifts a red stop sign while a car’s headlights glow softly through the fog behind, and points at a tablet showing a cloud icon and 5°C.',
  ask:'"What is fog made of?" "Why do we wear bright colours in fog?" "What do our ears do on a foggy walk?" "What is the most important fog rule of all?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "We are walking inside a cloud!" "Bright coats so cars can see us," or the rule, word perfect: "Stay close, hold hands, known paths."',
  real:'Watch the fog change your own street: the house you can see, the one you cannot, the bus that arrives like a ghost. Spot the fog workers — guards in bright vests, cars with lights on, ferries and trains sounding their horns. Fog dissolves by mid-morning: check the same view at lunch and celebrate the returning rooftops.',
  play:'Fog-game at home: greaseproof paper over a toy binoculars’ ends — how much can you spot now? Bright-survey in the wardrobe: which jackets would a foggy day love? Listening-walk with eyes closed (safely, sitting): what do your ears catch that your eyes missed? Then the torch test at dusk: one light on, one light off — which grown-up gets spotted first?',
  tips:'Keep fog explanations matter-of-fact and slightly magical — "walking inside a cloud" beats "limited visibility" every time. And if fog feels spooky, rehearse the route in sunshine first: the child who knows the three trees and the red door walks fog with confidence, because memory holds the map the mist hides.',
  check:'Ask: "What is fog? Why bright coats? What do we never do in fog?" A ground cloud, to be seen, and run-off-or-wander-off — the fog rules are now family rules.',
  safety:'Fog rules: stay together — hand in hand on known paths; wear or carry something bright; listen for cars you cannot see and cross only where a guard or clear view helps; never wander off to explore the greyness. Flashlights and reflective bits come out at dawn-and-dusk fog.'},
 'hot-weather':{
  title:'Hot Weather',one:'Sizzling-air days that ask for water, shade and cool routines — feeling the heat and outsmarting it.',
  seoTitle:'Hot Weather Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Drink-Rest-Shade Routine (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what hot weather feels like, the drink-rest-shade routine, water games that beat thirst and honest hot-body signals.',
  intro:'This lesson gives your child the hot-weather playbook: what the heat does to bodies, the drink-rest-shade routine that keeps play going all summer, the splashy ways to cool down, and the signals — dizzy, headachy — that always mean grown-up, now.',
  what:'Hot weather is when the air stays high-and-hot for days: strong sun overhead, warm wind, hot pavements, and everyone seeking shade, water and the cool side of the pillow.',
  mean:'Bodies work harder in heat — they sweat to stay cool, and sweating uses up water fast. That is why hot days rewrite the routine: more drinks, slower midday, lighter clothes, cooler games. The heat is not the enemy; running dry is.',
  know:'Your child needs three hot-weather ideas: hot bodies need water many times a day, not just when thirsty; the midday sun is the strongest, so shade and rests live at the middle of the day; and any dizzy, headachy or sick feeling means stop and tell a grown-up straight away.',
  why:'Hot weather is the first weather that can genuinely make a small body unwell, which makes it the most important self-care lesson in the class. A child who owns the drink-rest-shade routine — and knows that telling a grown-up about feeling unwell is brave, not whiny — carries a lifelong skill.',
  dev:'Interoception (reading one’s own body signals), routine planning around the sun, honest reporting as bravery, water-science through evaporation play, and the social rules of water safety that summer always rehearses.',
  vocab:'hot, shade, hydrate, sweat, cool down, dizzy, sunscreen, midday',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Hot weather means our bodies need extra help to stay cool and happy."\n2) Read the picture: the lifeguard by the pool wears a red sun hat, drinks from a big blue bottle, rubs sunscreen from an SPF 50 bottle onto his face, rests in the shade in a white chair, gives a thumbs-up from the pool wearing sunglasses, and holds a thermometer reading 30 degrees.\n3) Set the routine as three anchors: WATER often — bottle in hand, refills cheered; REST in shade when the sun is highest; HAT and sunscreen before the door.\n4) Teach the body signals with respect: dizzy, headachy, sick-in-the-heat means sit down, sip water, tell a grown-up immediately — that is not complaining, that is top-class body care.\n5) Plan one proper cool-down: paddling pool, water table, or a swim where a grown-up and a lifeguard are watching — always watching.',
  say:'"When the weather is very hot, we help our bodies: water many times a day, light clothes and a sun hat, rests in the shade when the sun is strongest, and cool water play with a grown-up watching. If anyone feels dizzy or unwell in the heat, we stop and tell a grown-up right away."',
  notice:'On this card a lifeguard in a white shirt and red shorts works at a sunny pool: he puts on a wide-brimmed red hat marked LIFEGUARD, drinks from a big blue water bottle, rubs sunscreen from a yellow SPF 50 bottle onto his cheek, relaxes in a white chair in the shade of a big umbrella, stands chest-deep in the pool giving a thumbs-up in dark sunglasses, and holds a white thermometer reading 30° with a little sun on it.',
  ask:'"What do our bodies need lots of on hot days?" "Where do we rest at midday?" "What does sunscreen do — and who puts it on?" "What do we say if we feel dizzy in the heat?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Water break!" unprompted at the park, "Midday is shade time," or the brave line practised once and kept forever: "I feel dizzy — I need a grown-up."',
  real:'Spot the heat routines all around: dogs stretched in shade, shop awnings out, the paddling pool in every second garden, the ice-cream queue. Feel the science: sun-side versus shade-side of a wall, hot car roof versus cool grass. Refill stations — which fountains and taps are on your walking map?',
  play:'Water-wall: cups, sponges and buckets against a fence — transfer games that soak everyone equally. Ice-rescue: freeze small toys in a tub and free them with warm water and spoons. Shade-fort build for the midday hours. Then the classic: a proper run under the sprinkler, towels on the line afterwards like signal flags.',
  tips:'Make drinking silly, not dutiful — silly-straw races, tick-charts, cheers at every refill. Freeze a bottle half-full then top it up for all-day cold. And protect the nap: hot, play-drunk children melt by late afternoon; shade the midday and the evening goes sweet instead of screamy.',
  check:'Ask: "What do hot bodies need? Where do we rest at midday? What do we say if we feel dizzy?" Water, shade, and grown-up-right-away — the heat is now managed, not feared.',
  safety:'Hot-weather rules: water often even without thirst; shade and rests at midday; hat and sunscreen applied by a grown-up; never anyone — child or pet — left in a parked car, not even for a minute; and any dizzy or sick feeling means stop, sit, sip and tell a grown-up at once.'},
 'warm-weather':{
  title:'Warm Weather',one:'The easy golden middle — light clothes, open windows and the season of effortless outdoor play.',
  seoTitle:'Warm Weather Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Light Clothes and Open-Air Days (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what warm weather feels like, the light-clothes switch, open-window days, garden play and the water-bottle habit that stays.',
  intro:'This lesson names the season children love best: the comfortable middle between cool and hot. What warm weather changes — clothes, windows, routines — and the simple habits (water bottle handy, hat for bright hours) that keep the golden days easy.',
  what:'Warm weather is comfortable mild heat: warmer than cool days, gentler than hot ones. Shirtsleeve and shorts weather, when windows stay open, play moves outdoors and nothing about going outside needs negotiating.',
  mean:'Warm weather changes whole households: heavier clothes go away, blankets thin out, windows open for fresh air, plants drink more, and the garden becomes the family’s best room. It is the easiest weather to be out in — which is exactly why the small habits still matter.',
  know:'Your child needs three warm-weather ideas: warm days ask for light clothes; houses get fresh air with open windows — feet stay on the floor near low ones; and the water bottle comes to every game, because warm-air play still drinks the body dry, just slowly.',
  why:'Warm weather is where weather routines become automatic — the training ground between the drama of hot and cold. A child who keeps the bottle-and-hat habits through the easy season owns them by the time the difficult seasons test them.',
  dev:'Seasonal sorting (what changes when warmth arrives), self-dressing in lighter layers, fresh-air awareness, steady hydration habits, and the observational joy of comparing the same room, the same walk, across two seasons.',
  vocab:'warm, mild, light clothes, fresh air, open window, garden, gentle, season',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Warm weather is the comfortable middle — not too hot, not too cold, just right for playing."\n2) Read the picture: the postman in a straw hat and light blue shirt waters pink and yellow flowers with a green can, drinks from his water bottle, opens a window from inside, kicks a football across the lawn, and points at a sunny forecast reading 28 degrees.\n3) Do the wardrobe switch together: which clothes are warm-weather clothes? Let your child sort the drawer — shorts here, jumpers there.\n4) Open the day like the card: window open, air in, plant watered. Feet on the floor by low windows — fresh air yes, climbing no.\n5) Anchor the bottle: warm-weather play still needs water. Bottle lives where the game lives, and refills are cheered.',
  say:'"Warm weather means it is easy to be outside — we wear our light clothes, we open the windows for fresh air, we water the flowers in the evening, and we keep our water bottle close while we play, because even easy weather makes us thirsty."',
  notice:'On this card a postman in a straw hat with a navy band, a light-blue short-sleeve shirt and navy shorts stands in a sunny garden: he adjusts his collar, waters pink and yellow flowers with a green watering can, drinks from a water bottle, opens a window beside a potted plant on the sill, runs kicking a black-and-white football across the grass, and points at a tablet showing a sun icon and 28 degrees.',
  ask:'"What do we wear in warm weather?" "What do we open to let fresh air in?" "What does the garden need more of in warm days?" "What stays with us at every game?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Warm day — shorts day!" "Window open, fresh air coming," or the gardener’s news: "I watered the flowers and the football match continued."',
  real:'Track the season’s changes on your own street: lighter clothes on the washing line, open windows up the road, the ice-cream van’s return, longer evenings. Compare photographs of the same walk in winter and in warmth — the trees, the clothes, the light, all different.',
  play:'Warm-evening games: the lawn becomes the stadium — football, skipping, chalk hopscotch, all in golden light. Flower-crew: your child owns the evening watering round with a small can. Window-report: each morning, is today a light-clothes day? The child who reports the season runs the season.',
  tips:'Warm days whisper rather than shout — keep the routines quietly alive: bottle filled, hat for the bright hours, window safety said once and held. And bank the ease: the child who self-manages water and hats in warm weather will re-learn the winter kit twice as fast.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we wear in warm weather? What do we open for fresh air? What comes to every game?" Light clothes, windows, the water bottle — the easy season is now fully managed.',
  safety:'Warm-day rules: water bottle within reach of every game; hat and shade for the brightest midday hours even when the air feels gentle; feet on the floor near open low windows — fresh air, no leaning; and evening watering with a watchful eye so the can waters flowers, not puddles indoors.'}
,
 'cool-weather':{
  title:'Cool Weather',one:'The fresh-nip season of one clever layer — zip-ups, warm drinks and walks with rosy cheeks.',
  seoTitle:'Cool Weather Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the One-Layer Rule and Zipping Skills (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what cool weather feels like, choosing one good layer, mastering the zip, warm-drink walks and the rosy-versus-shivery check.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the season between warm and cold: what the fresh nip in the air means, how one good layer solves it, the zip-up skill that makes jackets child-owned, and the honest cheek-check that says when inside is the right answer.',
  what:'Cool weather is fresh, chilly air — noticeably colder than warm, gentler than properly cold. You feel the nip at once, breath may show on the coolest mornings, and one comfortable jacket turns the whole day bright again.',
  mean:'Cool days are layer days: one zip-up layer, easy to shed when the sun arrives or the game heats up. Moving air makes cool feel cooler still — that is why bike riders and walkers zip right up. Cool weather is the training season for the deep cold to come.',
  know:'Your child needs three cool-weather ideas: cool air asks for one good layer — jacket, closed shoes; the zip is the child’s own job and own pride; and cheeks tell the truth — rosy is fine, shivery means inside for a warm-up.',
  why:'Cool weather is decision practice at the perfect difficulty: one layer, felt results. Zip it and feel warm, unzip when running, choose again tomorrow. That loop — decide, feel, adjust — is self-regulation in its most wearable form.',
  dev:'Self-dressing mastery (the zip!), temperature self-assessment, cause-and-effect with clothing choices, fine-motor control, and the independence glow of managing one’s own comfort.',
  vocab:'cool, crisp, jacket, zip, layer, warm drink, rosy, breeze',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Cool weather means the air has a fresh nip — one good jacket makes it perfect."\n2) Read the picture: the bike courier in a blue helmet zips his light blue jacket, ties his blue sneaker laces on a doorstep, holds a little thermometer that reads 72, carries a takeaway cup of warm drink, rides through the neighbourhood in a bike lane and points at a forecast reading 14 degrees.\n3) Learn the zip in stages: bottom teeth together, slider down, hold and pull — practise on the coat, then the jacket, until it is solo.\n4) Do the layer decision together at the door: peek outside, feel the air — jacket today? Which one? Your child picks from the hook.\n5) End with the cheek check on return: rosy and happy means the layer was right; shivery means we learn and layer up more next time.',
  say:'"Cool weather means the air is fresh and a bit chilly. We wear one good jacket — zipped by me! — and closed shoes. If we get warm playing, we can unzip. When we come home with rosy cheeks, that is the cool air saying we did it right."',
  notice:'On this card a bike courier in a blue bike helmet and a blue hooded jacket: zips the jacket up to his chin, crouches on a doorstep tying the laces of his blue trainers, holds up a small thermometer reading 72, carries a warm drink in a takeaway cup, rides his bicycle along a road with a painted bike lane, and points at a tablet forecast showing a partly-sunny icon and 14 degrees.',
  ask:'"What does cool air feel like?" "How many layers does a cool day need?" "Show me how your zip works." "What do rosy cheeks tell us?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Cool day — one layer!" "I zipped it all by myself," or the walk report: "My cheeks went rosy and I had a warm drink."',
  real:'Feel the cool-air difference on a bike or a fast buggy ride — moving air is colder air. Watch the zip-workers: riders, walkers, runners all zipping at the door. Keep the thermos ritual: one warm walk a week makes cool weather the cosy season.',
  play:'Zip-school: races on your own jacket, then helping teddy with his. Layer-sort relay: which clothes are cool-day clothes, which are cold-day ones? Warm-walk club: thermos of warm drink, crunchy leaves, and the mirror check at home — rosy cheeks earn the cocoa.',
  tips:'Resist dressing your child in two layers "to be safe" — one good layer with a felt result teaches more than double layers with none. And hand over the zip completely: the first solo zip of the season deserves the same celebration as a lost tooth.',
  check:'Ask: "How many layers for cool weather? Whose job is the zip? What do rosy cheeks mean?" One layer, mine, and all-is-well — cool weather is now child-managed.',
  safety:'Cool plus wind feels much colder — zip up for rides and walks, fingers covered on the chilliest days. Head in when shivers start; rosy is comfortable, shivery is a signal. And on the coolest mornings, breath-mist spotting is fun — but roads stay for walking, never for playing.'},
 'cold-weather':{
  title:'Cold Weather',one:'Frosty-air days of many layers — dressing deep, spotting ice and warming up like mountain experts.',
  seoTitle:'Cold Weather Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Layers, Ice Sense and Warm-Up Routines (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what cold weather does to bodies and puddles, the many-layers routine, ice-safety basics and the blanket-and-warm-drink finish.',
  intro:'This lesson takes on the serious cold: what freezing air does to bodies and puddles, how layers hold warmth in, the ice rules that keep small feet safe, and the warm-up routine — blanket, cocoa, radiator — that ends every cold-day outing like the mountain experts do.',
  what:'Cold weather is when the air drops close to or below freezing: breath turns to mist, puddles grow thin ice lids, fingers and ears feel the bite first, and snow may whiten the hills and rooftops.',
  mean:'Cold air steals body warmth — fastest from the bits that stick out: fingers, ears, noses, necks. Layers trap warm air close to the body, and each extra layer is another blanket of still air. Ice forms when water freezes: shiny, slippery, beautiful — and strictly for looking.',
  know:'Your child needs three cold-weather ideas: cold days need many layers — coat, hat, gloves, scarf — because layers hold our warmth; ice is shiny and slippery and never for walking on or touching with tongues; and the routine ends inside — blanket, warm drink, warm-up done.',
  why:'Cold weather is the deepest self-care lesson in the class: the body signals cold clearly (shivers, clumsy fingers) and the child learns to answer those signals with action — more layers or inside. Reading and answering one’s own body is the skill underneath all health habits.',
  dev:'Layer logic (trapped warm air), body-signal literacy (shivering, numb fingers), ice awareness, dressing sequences under real motivation, and the cosy discipline of the warm-up routine that ends every outing.',
  vocab:'cold, frost, ice, layers, shiver, numb, warm up, thermometer',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Cold weather means the air can make our bodies chilly fast — so we dress deep and warm up well."\n2) Read the picture: the mountain-rescue walker in a red jacket fastens it up, pulls on gloves, wraps a scarf, holds a thermometer reading minus 6, sits warm inside under a blanket with a hot drink by a wood stove, and checks a forecast showing minus 8 with snow.\n3) Build the layer picture with hands: one layer is one blanket of warm air; two layers is two blankets. How many blankets does today need?\n4) Set the ice rule like a rhyme: shiny means slippery — we look, we never lick, we never stamp. Puddles with lids are museum pieces.\n5) Drill the warm-up finish: come in, wet things off, blanket on, warm drink in hands. The outing is not done until the warm-up is done.',
  say:'"Cold weather means the air wants to take our warmth, so we wear many layers — coat, hat, gloves, scarf — like blankets we can walk in. Ice is shiny and slippery: we look at it, never stamp it. Then we come in, wrap up and get warm again. That is how cold days work."',
  notice:'On this card a mountain-rescue walker wears a red jacket with white cross patches and a dark beanie: fastens the jacket to the chin, pulls on a dark glove, wraps a navy scarf around the neck, holds a white thermometer reading −6°C, sits warm indoors under a blanket holding a mug beside a wood-burning stove, and points at a forecast tablet reading −8°C with snow icons by a mountain-rescue poster.',
  ask:'"Where does our body feel cold first?" "Why do we wear many layers instead of one big one?" "What is the rule about shiny ice?" "How does a cold-day outing end?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Two layers, two blankets of warm!" "Shiny means slippery — we look, never lick," or announce the finish like the experts: "Blanket, cocoa — warm-up complete!"',
  real:'Find the ice on the walk: puddle lids, windscreen frost, the shiny bit of the path the sun has not reached. Watch the layer-workers: builders, posties, dog walkers — all layered deep. Check the thermometer together every morning of the cold snap and let the number pick the number of blankets.',
  play:'Blanket-lab: wrap one teddy in one cloth, another in three — which stays cosiest? That is layers. Ice-look-but-not-touch: find three shiny puddle lids on the walk and rank their shine. Warm-up ritual as a game: wet kit off, blanket burrito, cocoa with two marshmallows — the child who owns the routine owns the season.',
  tips:'Warm children agree to come inside; cold-stunned ones melt. So set the come-in signals together before the outing: "When your fingers go clumsy or the shivers start, that is your body talking — we listen first time." And keep the warm-up ceremonial: the cocoa is not a snack, it is the finish line.',
  check:'Ask: "Why many layers? What do we never do with ice? How does a cold outing end?" Blankets-of-warm, look-never-lick, and blanket-cocoa-warm — the cold now has a system, and the system has your child.',
  safety:'Cold rules: cover fingers, ears and neck first; come in when shivering starts or fingers go clumsy. Ice is for looking — never for stamping, licking or walking on ponds or frozen water of any kind. Wet clothes get changed the moment we are home. Warm-up is part of the plan, not a treat.'},
 'spring':{
  title:'Spring',one:'The waking-up season — new leaves, returning birds, first seeds and showers that cannot make up their minds.',
  seoTitle:'Spring Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching New Growth, Tree Tracking and Shower-Ready Plans (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what spring changes week by week, adopt-a-tree tracking, cup-seed planting, returning birds and the rain-jacket habit for moody spring skies.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches spring as a story your child can watch weekly: what the season wakes up, how to adopt a tree and track its changes, how to plant the first seeds, which birds come back to sing — and why the rain jacket never quite goes away.',
  what:'Spring is the season of waking up: days stretch longer and warmer, buds burst into leaves, bulbs push up shoots, blossom covers the trees, and the birds that left for winter return singing and busy with nests.',
  mean:'Spring is change you can watch at child speed — one week buds, the next week leaves, the week after blossom. It is also a moody season: sunshine, shower, sunshine can share a single hour, which is why spring outings keep a rain jacket folded in the bag.',
  know:'Your child needs three spring ideas: spring is when plants wake and grow and birds come home; growth is fast and watchable — track one tree and see the story; and spring weather swings between sun and shower, so the light rain jacket travels with us.',
  why:'Spring is the season of patient observation: the child who visits the same tree every week learns that big changes are made of small weekly changes — the deepest idea in science, taught by a bud. Add planting a seed and the season teaches cause, patience and care in one pot.',
  dev:'Weekly observation and recording, growth sequencing, plant-care responsibility, bird recognition and listening, weather flexibility (sun-shower-sun in one outing), and the vocabulary of new life: bud, shoot, blossom, nest, shoot, seedling.',
  vocab:'spring, bud, blossom, seed, shoot, nest, shower, grow',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Spring is when the world wakes up — the trees, the flowers, the birds."\n2) Read the picture: the gardener in a straw hat plants seeds in the soft soil, touches the new green leaves on a branch, wears a yellow rain jacket under a grey shower, watches a robin and a blue bird on a blossom branch, waters the tulips with a green can and points at a partly-sunny forecast reading 18 degrees.\n3) Adopt a tree today: your tree for the whole spring. Visit weekly, draw what changed — bud, leaf, blossom — and keep the drawings in order like a comic strip.\n4) Plant the first seeds: beans in a clear cup by the window. Watch the root wiggle down, the shoot push up. Measure it with your finger every Monday.\n5) Pack the spring bag: sun hat AND folded rain jacket. Say the spring rule out loud: in spring we carry both, because the sky changes its mind.',
  say:'"Spring is the waking-up season. The trees grow new leaves, the flowers push up, the birds come back to sing and build nests, and we plant our seeds in the soft ground. We keep our rain jackets close, because spring skies love a quick shower between the sunshine."',
  notice:'On this card a gardener in a straw hat with a green band, a light-green shirt and green overalls: kneels to plant seeds beside a seed packet with tulips blooming nearby, reaches up to touch fresh new leaves on a branch, wears a yellow rain jacket under a grey shower cloud, sits on a bench watching a robin and a small blue bird on a blossom branch, waters pink tulips and white daisies with a green watering can, and points at a tablet showing a partly-sunny icon and 18 degrees.',
  ask:'"What is waking up in spring?" "What did OUR tree change this week?" "What does a seed need to start growing?" "Why do we carry a rain jacket in spring?"',
  childSay:'Your child might report like a scientist: "Two new leaves on our tree — three last week plus one!" or the planting log: "My bean grew a shoot as tall as my finger."',
  real:'Walk the neighbourhood as a bud-census: which trees woke first? Count the returning birds and learn one song by heart — the one that sings outside your window. Find the first bees back at work on the blossom and watch them a respectful moment: spring has staff, and they are busy.',
  play:'Tree-diary comic strip: one square per week, taped in a row — the tree’s whole spring as a story. Seed race: bean versus sunflower in neighbouring cups — who sprouts first, who grows tallest? Nest-watch from a distance with binoculars: busy birds, never touched, always marvellous.',
  tips:'Keep the tree visits short and sacred — five minutes, same tree, same question: "What changed?" The discipline is the magic. And when the shower ambushes your picnic, laugh and shelter: getting caught by a spring shower once teaches the jacket rule better than a hundred reminders.',
  check:'Ask: "What happens in spring? What are we tracking on our tree? Why carry a rain jacket?" Waking-up, the weekly changes, and moody skies — your child is now fluent in spring.',
  safety:'Spring rules: hats return with the blossom — spring sunshine still burns; wash hands after soil and watering cans before snacks; watch nests and baby birds from a distance, never touch; and on blossom-branch climbs, remember wet spring grass is slippery grass.'},
 'summer':{
  title:'Summer',one:'The long-bright season — beach flags, sun hats, swim-with-a-grown-up rules and evenings that stay golden.',
  seoTitle:'Summer Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Beach Flags and the Sun-Safe Summer (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what makes summer different, the hat-sunscreen-water routine, beach flags and swim-with-a-grown-up rules, and shade-fort living at midday.',
  intro:'This lesson gives summer its structure: the longest, brightest days, the hat-sunscreen-water routine that never takes a day off, the swim rules that matter most, and how to read a beach flag like a local — all so the golden season stays pure joy.',
  what:'Summer is the warmest season: the longest days and strongest sun, holidays from school, the beach and the pool at their best, fruit ripening everywhere and daylight stretching right into the evening.',
  mean:'Summer fun runs on summer rules. The sun is strong all day, so hats, sunscreen and shade at midday are how the day works — not optional extras. Swimming happens where lifeguards watch, between the flags. And the long light plays tricks: bedtime says sleep while the sky says play.',
  know:'Your child needs three summer ideas: summer sun is strong — hat, sunscreen by a grown-up and water all day, every day; we swim only where a watching grown-up and the lifeguards can see us, between the red-and-yellow flags; and the strongest-sun hours are for shade forts, slow games and long cool drinks.',
  why:'Summer carries the season’s biggest safety stakes and its biggest joys in the same hours. The child who learns the flag system, the watch-me-swim rule and the midday-shade rhythm at four and five owns them for every summer after — this is the class’s most lasting lesson wearing its happiest face.',
  dev:'Rule ownership (flags, swim-with-a-grown-up), routine persistence through holiday looseness, time concepts through daylight tracking, water confidence with supervision, and the self-knowledge of reading one’s own thirst, heat and tiredness.',
  vocab:'summer, lifeguard, flag, sunscreen, midday, shade, paddle, ripened',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Summer is the season of long bright days — and the sun plays by strong rules."\n2) Read the picture: the beach-safety lifeguard in a yellow hat and shirt waves on the sand beside a red-and-yellow flag on a pole and a rescue board; the panels show her adjusting the sun hat, rubbing sunscreen from a bottle, drinking water, swimming in the sea with a small child, resting in a striped chair under an umbrella and pointing at a sunny forecast reading 29 degrees.\n3) Learn the beach flags like treasure signs: red-and-yellow means lifeguards watch this stretch — we swim between the flags, always in sight of our grown-up.\n4) Fix the routine as a song: hat, sunscreen, water bottle — every day, no holidays from the routine, even when the day feels easy.\n5) Plan the midday like the card: the strong-sun hours are for shade — a fort, a striped chair, a long drink — then back out when the sun mellows.',
  say:'"Summer is the long, bright, beach season. We wear our hats, we drink water all day, we rest in the shade when the sun is strongest — and we swim only where a grown-up is watching and the red-and-yellow flag says the lifeguards can see us. Then summer is all joy, all season."',
  notice:'On this card a beach-safety lifeguard in a yellow wide-brim hat and yellow "beach safety" shirt stands waving on the sand: behind her fly a red-and-yellow flag on a pole and a yellow rescue board marked RESCUE, with a lifeguard chair and a striped umbrella nearby; the panels show her adjusting her hat, rubbing sunscreen from a bottle onto her cheek, drinking from a bottle, standing waist-deep in the sea with a small boy, dozing happily in a striped beach chair under an umbrella, and pointing at a phone forecast reading 29° with a sun icon and the word Sunny.',
  ask:'"What do the red-and-yellow flags mean?" "Who watches us when we swim?" "What are the midday hours for?" "What never takes a holiday in summer?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Between the flags, near my grown-up!" "Midday is shade-fort time," or the routine anthem: "Hat, sunscreen, bottle — every day!"',
  real:'Read the flags at every beach and pool visit — red-and-yellow for swimming, and ask the lifeguard together what today means. Spot the summer routines on the sand: hats, umbrellas, cool boxes, the lifeguard’s high chair. Track the long light: what time does the sun set this week — and next?',
  play:'Flag-game in the garden: one child waves red-and-yellow — swimming happens "between" two towels, grown-up in the middle. Shade-fort engineering contest for the midday hours. Fruit-ice lolly kitchen: freeze juice with berries. And the summer classic: an evening walk in the golden light, tracking how late the shadows stretch.',
  tips:'Hold the routine through the holiday looseness — summer is when routines quietly dissolve, so anchor just three (hat, water, swim-with-a-watching-grown-up) and defend them warmly. And answer "why?" fully once: children follow rules they understand; "so the lifeguard can always see you" beats "because" every time.',
  check:'Ask: "What do the flags mean? Who watches at swim time? What never takes a summer holiday?" Lifeguards-watch, my-grown-up, and hat-sunscreen-water — the golden season is now safe to love completely.',
  safety:'Summer’s big rules: never swim without a watching grown-up, only between the flags where lifeguards watch; hats and sunscreen every day, shade at midday; never sit in a parked car; and come to a grown-up at once for any dizzy, headachy or sick feeling in the heat.'},
 'autumn':{
  title:'Autumn',one:'The leaf-falling season — gold and orange trees, raking jobs, leaf collections and hats in the wind.',
  seoTitle:'Autumn Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Leaf Seasons, Collections and Windy-Day Care (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what autumn changes, leaf collecting and sorting, raking games, the sweater switch and holding hats on blustery autumn days.',
  intro:'This lesson turns autumn into the collecting season: why the leaves change and fall, how to build a leaf collection worth keeping, the raking-and-jumping tradition, the sweater switch in the wardrobe — and why autumn wind keeps taking hats.',
  what:'Autumn is the season after summer: days shorten, air cools, and deciduous trees stop making green, letting gold, orange and red show instead — then let the leaves go, one gust at a time.',
  mean:'Autumn is the great change-and-fall season: leaves stop making their green food, show their hidden colours, then drop to feed the soil. The cooling air asks for sweaters. The season’s winds pick up — which is why autumn walks come with hat-holding.',
  know:'Your child needs three autumn ideas: leaves change colour because the tree is resting for winter, then fall to feed the ground; autumn air asks for sweaters and light layers; and autumn wind is playful — hats get held, and leaf heaps get jumped in.',
  why:'Autumn is the classification season: leaves by colour, shape, size, tree. Sorting a real, beautiful collection is early-maths and early-science in one afternoon. The visible cooling also ties calendar to wardrobe — the child who packs away the summer clothes has understood the season, not just watched it.',
  dev:'Sorting and classifying with real objects, seasonal cause-and-effect (less sun, cooler days, colour change), collection-building and care, wardrobe switching as life-admin practice, and gross-motor joy in raking and jumping.',
  vocab:'autumn, leaf, gold, rake, sweater, gust, crunch, harvest',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Autumn is when the leaves turn gold and orange and fall down for a rest."\n2) Read the picture: the school caretaker in a cap rakes leaves into a heap by the school, wears a light grey sweater, holds up maple leaves in orange and yellow, kneels to collect leaves into a big green bag, holds his cap on in the wind as leaves fly, and points at a forecast reading 15°C.\n3) Start the collection: gather ten fallen leaves — different colours, shapes, sizes. Sort them at the kitchen table: by colour, then size, then shape. Keep the best in a heavy-book press.\n4) Do the sweater switch: which clothes are autumn clothes? Let your child move the summer things away and lay out the jumpers.\n5) Practise hat-holding: autumn wind tests hats. Hand on head at the gusty bits — and if it blows off, we chase it, laughing, and hold on tighter.',
  say:'"Autumn is the leaf season. The trees turn gold and orange, then let their leaves go — the wind helps them fly. We wear our sweaters, we rake the leaves into heaps, we collect the prettiest ones, and we hold our hats when the autumn wind comes dancing."',
  notice:'On this card a school caretaker in a navy cap, light-grey zip sweater and navy trousers works around a school building: he rakes orange leaves into a heap beside an orange-leafed tree, stands smiling in his sweater, holds up two maple leaves — one orange, one golden — kneels packing leaves into a tall green garden bag, steadies his cap with one hand as leaves swirl past a playground slide, and points at a tablet forecast showing a cloud icon and 15°C.',
  ask:'"Why do the leaves change colour?" "What did we put in our collection today?" "What do we wear now the air is cooler?" "What does the autumn wind love to take?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Our tree went full gold this week!" "Sweater weather — I picked the blue one," or the wind report: "I held my hat with ONE hand — the wind tried!"',
  real:'Walk the leaf-line: which trees turned first, which are still green? Find the leaf-workers — caretakers, sweepers, the wind itself. Visit a market or farm at harvest time and name what autumn ripened: apples, pumpkins, plums. Notice the darker mornings — autumn is shortening the days.',
  play:'Leaf-olympics: best colour, biggest, funniest shape, most points. Rake-and-jump: a proper heap, a proper jump, a proper repeat. Leaf-art: rubbings with crayons over the ridges, collages of the collection. And the tree-watch begins: adopt your tree now, so winter’s bare branches are a story, not a surprise.',
  tips:'Let the collection be the child’s — messy, abundant, curated by enthusiasm rather than by adult taste. The heavy-book press is the museum, and the museum belongs to them. And join the jump: one grown-up cannonball into the leaf heap makes the whole season memorable.',
  check:'Ask: "What happens to the leaves in autumn? What do we wear? What does the wind take?" Change-and-fall, sweaters, and hats — the golden season is officially collected.',
  safety:'Autumn rules: dewy and leafy slopes are slippery — walking feet down leafy banks; shorter, darker days mean something bright on jackets for the roadside walk; and leaf piles at the kerb are never for jumping — garden heaps only, where no traffic passes.'},
 'winter':{
  title:'Winter',one:'The cold-and-quiet season — full winter kit, frosty careful walks, sheltered waits and cosy returns.',
  seoTitle:'Winter Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Full Kit Sequence and Winter Routines (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what winter brings, the full dressing sequence, careful frosty walking, sheltered bus-stop waits and the warm-up that ends every outing.',
  intro:'This lesson assembles winter the way the card does — coat, scarf, gloves, then the careful outside world: frosty paths walked carefully, bus stops waited in shelter, the early dark noticed — and the blanket-cocoa finish that makes winter feel mastered, not endured.',
  what:'Winter is the coldest season: the shortest days, the longest nights, bare trees, frosty mornings and, in many places, snow and ice. Everyone finds warm ways through — coats and fires for people, dens and thick fur for animals.',
  mean:'Winter rewrites daily life: dressing takes longer and more layers, walking gets careful on frosty ground, waiting happens in sheltered spots, and the dark arrives early. Winter is also the season of warm returns — the house glows brighter the colder the street.',
  know:'Your child needs three winter ideas: winter asks for the full kit — warm coat, scarf, gloves, thick boots — before any door opens; frosty paths need careful small steps; and every winter outing ends with the warm-up: wet things off, warmth on, drink in hands.',
  why:'Winter is self-care at its most visible: the child who can dress for winter, walk on frost and warm up afterwards has mastered the year’s hardest routine. And the early dark, met with lantern walks and cosy rituals, becomes atmosphere instead of fear.',
  dev:'Full multi-item sequencing, careful motor control on frost, patience with the long dressing routine, day-length awareness (dark at teatime!), and the emotional pattern of effort-then-cosiness that winter teaches best.',
  vocab:'winter, frost, coat, gloves, scarf, shelter, dusk, warm up',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Winter is the coldest season — we dress deep and walk careful."\n2) Read the picture: the bus driver in a navy fur-hooded coat fastens it, wraps a long blue scarf, pulls on blue gloves, walks carefully on the snowy pavement past a blue bus, waits with hands in pockets under a "BUS STOP" shelter, and points at a forecast reading TODAY minus 2 degrees with snowflakes.\n3) Drill the full kit in order, every time: coat, then scarf, then hat, then gloves, then boots — each piece named out loud like a roll-call.\n4) Practise the frost walk: small flat steps, knees soft, hands free — on the rug, then on the real path. Shiny means careful.\n5) Build the warm-up finish as a ritual: wet things off, blanket on, warm drink, check the cheeks. Winter outings end in cosiness, always.',
  say:'"Winter is the coldest season, so we wear the full kit — coat, scarf, hat, gloves, boots — before we open any door. We take small careful steps on frosty paths, we wait for the bus in the shelter, and when we come home, we warm up: blanket, cocoa, cosy. That is how winter is done."',
  notice:'On this card a bus driver with curly dark hair wears a navy winter coat with a fur-trimmed hood: fastens the coat with a hand in the pocket, wraps a long blue scarf around her neck, pulls on a blue glove, walks carefully along a snowy pavement with a blue city bus behind, waits calmly with hands in pockets under a bus-stop shelter marked BUS STOP, and points at a tablet reading TODAY with a snow-cloud icon and −2°.',
  ask:'"What is the winter roll-call?" (Coat, scarf, hat, gloves, boots.) "How do we walk on frost?" "Where do we wait for the bus?" "How does every winter outing end?"',
  childSay:'Your child might run the roll-call solo: "Coat! Scarf! Hat! Gloves! Boots — check!" report the pavement: "Frosty bit — small steps," or declare the finish: "Blanket, cocoa — winter done right."',
  real:'Track the season’s signals: darker mornings, darker teatimes, breath-clouds at the bus stop, frost on the grass that melts by lunch. Watch the winter workers — drivers, posties, gritters — keeping the town moving. Notice the animal plans: fat birds at the feeder, cats curled on warm windowsills.',
  play:'Dressing-race against the clock — beat yesterday’s roll-call time. Lantern walk at dusk with a real lantern or torch: make the early dark a treat. Winter den: the blanket fort with books and cocoa where all frosty outings end. And the window-frost gallery: breathe on the glass and draw in the mist.',
  tips:'Keep winter outings short and double-batched: twenty brisk minutes out, full warm-up, and out again if the spirit is willing. The dressing effort is the day’s biggest ask — celebrate the roll-call like a trophy. And make the dark friendly early: lanterns, torches and window-glow turn dusk into winter’s own story-time.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the winter kit. How do frosty paths get walked? How do outings end?" The full roll-call, small careful steps, and blanket-cocoa — winter is now a routine your child runs.',
  safety:'Winter rules: the full kit before every door, no exceptions — fingers, ears and necks chill first. Small steps on frost and ice, never running on shiny ground, ice on ponds strictly for looking. Something bright or reflective for the dark walks, and warm-up inside before the shivers turn to shivers-plus.'}
,
 'umbrella':{
  title:'Umbrella',one:'Your own foldable rain roof — opening it high, carrying it kindly and folding it safely.',
  seoTitle:'Umbrella Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Safe Opening, Carrying and Folding (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: the parts of an umbrella, the open-up-high rule, spoke safety in crowds, safe folding and the doorstep spot where umbrellas belong.',
  intro:'This lesson masters the umbrella from fold to fold: naming its parts, the open-outdoors rule, holding it high so it can shed rain, the spoke-safety courtesy for crowded pavements, and the satisfying click of folding it away dry.',
  what:'An umbrella is a folding canopy on a handle: fabric stretched over ribbed spokes that opens into a small round roof and folds back into a slim stick you carry until the rain starts.',
  mean:'The umbrella works by shedding: rain lands on the sloped fabric and slides off, while you stay dry in the middle. That is why we hold it up high — the roof needs its shape and its height. The spokes that give the roof its curve are also its pointy bits, and they ask for room and respect.',
  know:'Your child needs three umbrella ideas: umbrellas open outdoors only, at the doorstep spot; we hold them up high and away from eyes — ours and other people’s; and when the rain ends we fold them carefully and let them dry by the door.',
  why:'An umbrella is a child’s first piece of equipment with real moving parts and a real safety edge — the spokes. Learning to open, carry and fold it is fine-motor skill, sequencing and courtesy all in one object, and the confidence of carrying your own roof is enormous.',
  dev:'Mechanical sequencing (open, hold, fold), fine-motor control, spatial awareness of sharp points around others, responsibility for equipment, and the social grace of keeping one’s spokes to oneself on a crowded street.',
  vocab:'umbrella, canopy, spokes, handle, fold, shelter, rain roof, dry',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "An umbrella is a little rain roof you carry with you."\n2) Read the picture: the bus driver at a rainy bus stop opens his navy umbrella, holds it up high beside a small girl in a yellow raincoat with a pink backpack, keeps clear space around the open canopy, folds it with the strap, leans it by the bus door and checks a phone showing a rain cloud and 70%.\n3) Learn the parts together: canopy (the roof), spokes (the ribs underneath), runner (the slide that opens it), handle (where we hold). Open one slowly and count the spokes.\n4) Set the two rules as a pair: umbrellas open OUTDOORS, at our doorstep spot — and we keep them up high and away from eyes, including on the crowded pavement, where we hold them still and give everyone room.\n5) Practise the fold until it clicks: close, smooth the fabric, wrap the strap, stand it by the door to dry. Done well, it is deeply satisfying.',
  say:'"The umbrella is our own little rain roof. We open it outdoors at the doorstep, we hold it up high so the rain rolls right off, and we keep the pointy spokes away from everyone — big swings are for playgrounds, not pavements. When the rain stops, we fold it, strap it and let it dry by the door."',
  notice:'On this card a bus driver in a navy cap, light-blue shirt and tie stands at a rainy bus stop with a blue bus behind: he opens a folded navy umbrella, holds a wide open umbrella up high beside a small child in a yellow raincoat with a pink backpack, holds the closed umbrella tilted down with clear space around it, fastens the strap on the folded canopy, leans the folded umbrella by the bus door, and points at a phone showing a rain-cloud icon and 70%.',
  ask:'"What are the parts of our umbrella?" "Where do umbrellas open — and where never?" "Why do we hold it up high?" "How do we carry it when the pavement gets busy?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Open at the doorstep, up high, away from eyes!" count the spokes proudly, or report the fold: "Strapped and drying — click!"',
  real:'Watch umbrella manners on a wet school run: who gives room, who pokes, who jabs through gate gaps — and be the family that does it right. Spot the big versions: market umbrellas, café parasols, the flat canopies over bus stops. Notice where wet umbrellas live indoors: stands, trays, hooks by every good door.',
  play:'Umbrella school on the doorstep: open, hold high, walk a line of teddies giving everyone room, fold, strap, stand to dry — full certification ceremony included. Canopy-count: how many spokes on ours? Then the rainy-day parade: favourite toys ride under the real umbrella on the wet pavement, perfectly dry and thoroughly delighted.',
  tips:'Give the rule a place, not just a no: "Umbrellas open at the doorstep" works because the doorstep is satisfying and legal. Choose a child umbrella with a blunt tip and a runner that needs a push-and-button (not a flick) — safer mechanics for keener hands.',
  check:'Ask: "Where do umbrellas open? How do we carry them in a crowd? What happens when the rain stops?" Doorstep, up-high-and-away, fold-and-dry — your child now carries their own roof, courteously.',
  safety:'Umbrella safety: open outdoors only, away from every eye; keep the pointy spokes still and spaced in crowds; never poke, jab or sword-fight; mind the runner so small fingers do not pinch; and on gusty days the umbrella stays home — wind flips canopies, and the raincoat wins those days.'},
 'raincoat':{
  title:'Raincoat',one:'The full dry-kit ritual — arms, zip, hood, sleeves — and the peg where the coat dries for next time.',
  seoTitle:'Raincoat Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Zip-Hood-Sleeves Sequence (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a raincoat does, the four-step wearing sequence, hood practice that sticks, sleeve checks and the hang-it-to-dry habit.',
  intro:'This lesson makes the raincoat routine automatic: what waterproof fabric does, the four moves — arms in, zip up, hood on, sleeves down — the hood practice that wins over hood-haters, and the hang-it-on-the-peg finish that keeps tomorrow’s coat dry.',
  what:'A raincoat is a coat made of waterproof fabric with a hood and a zip: rain lands on it, beads up and slides off, while the clothes underneath stay completely dry.',
  mean:'The raincoat works as a system — the hood saves the head, the zip seals the front, the sleeves cover the wrists, the pockets flap over their openings. Miss one part and the rain finds it: the unzipped collar, the pushed-up sleeve, the bare head in a downpour.',
  know:'Your child needs three raincoat ideas: the raincoat keeps clothes dry so bodies stay warm; the sequence is arms in, zip to the top, hood up, sleeves down — all four, every rainy day; and after the rain, the coat hangs on its peg to dry, ready for next time.',
  why:'The raincoat is the gateway garment of self-dressing: the zip is a milestone, the hood is an sensory hurdle, the sequence is memory work — and unlike the winter coat, it is light enough to practise daily. A child who owns the raincoat routine walks to preschool as the boss of their own dryness.',
  dev:'Four-step sequencing, zip fine-motor work, sensory tolerance (the hood!), responsibility for drying and stowing, and the pride loop of staying dry because of what I did myself.',
  vocab:'raincoat, waterproof, zip, hood, sleeves, peg, dry, drizzle',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "The raincoat is your waterproof shell — rain knocks, but it cannot come in."\n2) Read the picture: the teacher with a bun and a green cardigan picks the yellow raincoat from a hanger on the coat rack, slips it on over her cardigan, zips it to the top, pulls the hood over her head, checks her sleeves are down, and hangs it back on the peg rack to dry by the door.\n3) Teach the sequence as four beats with actions: ARMS (both in), ZIP (bottom tooth to the top), HOOD (over the head), SLEEVES (pulled down past the wrists). Chant it on drizzly mornings until it runs itself.\n4) Practise the hood gently, never in a rush: hood-up in a mirror game, hood-up on teddy, hood-up for one slow count of three — tolerance grows in play, not in panic.\n5) End the ritual at the peg: after the rain, the coat hangs up, zipped open to air, hood flipped out. Tomorrow’s dry coat is tonight’s small job.',
  say:'"My raincoat keeps the rain off my clothes. Arms in, zip to my chin, hood over my head, sleeves down past my wrists — four moves and I am waterproof. After the rain, my coat hangs on its peg to dry, ready for the next grey morning."',
  notice:'On this card a teacher with dark hair in a bun, wearing a green cardigan over a white top: chooses the yellow raincoat from a hanger on a wooden rack with a blue and a pink coat behind, slips her arms in at the blue door, zips it up to the collar, pulls the hood on over her bun, checks the sleeves down to her wrists while standing by the open door in the rain, and hangs the coat on the peg rack to dry with her back to us.',
  ask:'"What are the four raincoat moves?" "Which part keeps the rain off your head?" "Why does the coat hang on the peg after rain?" "What happens if we forget the zip?"',
  childSay:'Your child might chant it solo: "Arms, zip, hood, sleeves!" report from the mirror: "Hood up — I can still see!" or do the finish unprompted: coat on the peg, hood out, "Ready for next time."',
  real:'Spot the raincoat system at preschool pickup: every good coat rack has hooks at child height and space for wet coats to air. Watch the zip-workers at the door. Notice who is dry by lunchtime — the four-moves crowd — and who arrived soaked: the collar-open, hood-forgotten crowd. The sequence is the difference.',
  play:'Four-moves race on a dry day: arms-zip-hood-sleeves against the clock, then teaching the sequence to teddy. Hood-desensitising game: peek-a-boo hoods in the mirror, then hood-up for a slow three-count, then a hood-up dance move. Peg-practice: the hang-it-dry routine as a fine-motor challenge — coat on the hook, hood out, zip open to air. Certification: your child runs the door-check for the whole family on the next drizzle.',
  tips:'The hood is the hill to take slowly — never force it down in a hurry, because a hood fight at the door poisons the whole routine. Practise in play, choose coats with hoods your child likes, and accept hood-today-hair-tomorrow. And keep the peg low: the drying step sticks only when the hook is within reach.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the four moves. Why the hood? What do we do with the coat after rain?" Arms-zip-hood-sleeves, head-dry, peg-to-dry — the routine now belongs to your child.',
  safety:'Raincoat rules: all four moves before the door, in real rain — the damp-drizzle compromise is how colds catch rides. Check the zip does not catch chin skin, and the hood tie (if any) is not a long loose cord. Hang wet coats to dry properly: a damp coat tomorrow is a shivery child by lunchtime.'},
 'rain-boots':{
  title:'Rain Boots',one:'Waterproof feet for wet ground — the pull-on wiggle, the sole check and the puddle map.',
  seoTitle:'Rain Boots Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Pull-On Wiggle and Puddle Judgement (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what rain boots do, the pull-on wiggle with the heel check, checking soles, puddle mapping and the tray-by-the-door drying habit.',
  intro:'This lesson makes the boots routine child-owned from pull to peg: what waterproof boots do, the pull-on wiggle with the heel-all-the-way-down check, the sole inspection before the wet pavement, puddle judgement — splashers versus monsters — and the tray by the door that ends the wet-sock era.',
  what:'Rain boots are tall waterproof boots of rubber or plastic, worn over socks on wet days. They rise above the ankle so shallow water stays out, and their ridged soles grip wet, shiny ground.',
  mean:'Boots change the maths of a rainy day: wet ground becomes walkable, puddles become toys instead of hazards, and feet stay warm because they stay dry. The tall shaft guards against the shallow slosh; the sole guards against the slip. Both deserve a quick check before the door.',
  know:'Your child needs three rain-boot ideas: boots go over socks — the wiggle test makes sure heels are down and toes can wiggle; soles get a quick check for grip and stones before wet ground; and puddles are judged — small splashers are for stepping in, deep monsters and kerb-side ones are for stepping around.',
  why:'Boots are usually the first weather garment a child masters alone — which makes them the confidence engine of the whole wet-day routine. And puddle judgement is genuine risk assessment at child scale: which water, how deep, where does it lead? That thinking grows up into every safety decision after.',
  dev:'Independent dressing (the wiggle), observation and comparison (soles, puddle depth), risk judgement at a safe scale, responsibility for kit (tray, drying), and the pure gross-motor delight of sanctioned splashing.',
  vocab:'rain boots, wellies, waterproof, wiggle, sole, grip, puddle, splash',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Rain boots are waterproof feet — wet ground cannot get us now."\n2) Read the picture: the crossing guard in a yellow high-visibility jacket chooses yellow boots from a shop shelf of colours, sits on a bench pulling them on, kneels to check a boot sole on the wet pavement, steps carefully around a puddle at the zebra crossing, gives a thumbs-up under her umbrella, and sets the boots on a black tray by the door at home.\n3) Master the wiggle: sit, point the toe, push, then wiggle — heel all the way down, toes free. Do the squat-check: a little jump — did the heel come up? Then down again and re-wiggle.\n4) Check the soles like the guard: flip one boot — is the grip pattern clear of stones? Slippery soles slip; grippy soles grip. Feel the difference on the doormat.\n5) Draw the puddle map after the next rain: splashers (green circles) and monsters (red Xs) on your street. Walk it together — child as the map-reader.',
  say:'"Rain boots are waterproof feet for wet days. Socks on, toe in, push and wiggle — heel down, toes free. We check our soles for grip, we splash the small puddles, we step around the deep ones and never at the road. After, the boots live on their tray by the door, drying for tomorrow."',
  notice:'The rain-boots card follows the crossing guard in her navy cap and bright yellow high-visibility jacket: she picks yellow rain boots from a shop shelf stacked with green, pink, purple and navy pairs, sits on a bench by a blue door pulling a yellow boot on, kneels on wet pavement pointing at a boot sole beside a pedestrian-crossing sign, steps carefully around a puddle while holding her stop sign, stands under a navy umbrella giving a thumbs-up in the rain, and places both boots on a black tray by a door where a pink coat hangs on a hook.',
  ask:'"What does the wiggle check?" "Why do we look at our soles?" "Which puddles may we splash?" "Where do the boots live after the rain?"',
  childSay:'Your child might report: "Heel down, toes wiggling — good to go!" grade the street: "That is a splasher, that one is a MONSTER," or finish the routine: "Boots on the tray — dry for tomorrow."',
  real:'Visit a boot shop and let your child pick from two joyful options — ownership starts at the shelf. Check real soles after a wet week: worn grips versus new grips, felt on the doormat. Study the big puddles on your street after rain: where do they form, why there, and which are always monsters?',
  play:'Wiggle-school with a jump-check finish. Puddle-map cartography after the next rain — green circles and red Xs, laminated if possible, consulted proudly on every wet walk. Sole-compare: grippy versus smooth on the doormat. And the sanctioned splash finale: full kit, chosen splasher, one mighty two-footed jump — socks stay dry, and the child knows exactly why.',
  tips:'Boots over bare feet cause rubs — the sock rule is non-negotiable and worth saying once, clearly. Let the colour be loud: the child who adores their boots asks to go out in rain. And hold the line on monsters: the map works because the judgements are real, and real judgement needs a real no sometimes.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the wiggle do? Why check soles? Which puddles are never for splashing?" Heel-down, grip-and-stones, and kerb-side-never — waterproof feet, fully managed.',
  safety:'Boot safety: socks always under boots; heels all the way down or the trip risk doubles; soles checked for worn grip and lodged stones. Puddles: splashers yes, kerb-side and road-edge puddles never, and nothing deeper than the boot top — monsters are for looking, not wading.'},
 'sun-hat':{
  title:'Sun Hat',one:'A brim that makes its own shade — choosing the hat, checking the fit and wearing the cool.',
  seoTitle:'Sun Hat Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Hat Routine and the Fit Check (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a sun hat does, choosing from the shelf, the brim-and-strap fit check, the shade-sail rest and the water-bottle pairing that beats the heat.',
  intro:'This lesson turns the sun hat from a wrestle into a routine: what the brim actually does, choosing the hat that the child will actually wear, the fit check — brim level, strap snug — and the hat-plus-shade-plus-water pairing that keeps summer play going strong.',
  what:'A sun hat is a hat with a wide brim all the way around — its job is to make shade: over the face, the ears, the neck. Many have a chin strap or cord so games and gusts cannot take it.',
  mean:'The sun hat is wearable shade. Put it on and the head instantly sits in its own cool patch — you can feel the difference in one second. That felt feedback is the whole trick: the hat sells itself to the skin, if we let the child feel it work.',
  know:'Your child needs three sun-hat ideas: the brim makes shade for face, ears and neck; the fit check is brim level, strap snug — snug for safety, not tight; and the hat works as a team with shade breaks and the water bottle — the summer three.',
  why:'Hat routines built on felt comfort and child choice survive every summer after — hat routines built on nagging dissolve by June. This lesson is as much about letting the child own the gear as it is about the gear itself, and ownership is the only sun protection that does not need reapplying.',
  dev:'Self-care routine ownership, fit-and-function reasoning (why the brim, why the strap), choice-making within limits, the felt science of shade versus sun, and the habit-stacking of hat-plus-bottle as one summer gesture.',
  vocab:'sun hat, brim, shade, strap, fit, cool, playground, bright',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "The sun hat is shade you wear — your own travelling patch of cool."\n2) Read the picture: the playground supervisor in a yellow shirt chooses a blue sun hat from a shelf of yellow, pink, green and beige hats, puts it on by the playground, checks the fit with her hands at the brim and the chin strap, sits on a bench in the shade of a big blue sail, drinks from a blue water bottle and holds a thermometer reading 26°C.\n3) Do the felt test: ten seconds in the sun hatless — then hat on. "Cooler, right?" Let the skin argue for the hat.\n4) Learn the fit check as two hands: one levels the brim over the eyes, one snaps the strap — snug enough for a finger, no looser. Now jump: does it stay?\n5) Pair the routine: hat on, bottle filled — the summer three (hat, water, shade) as one door-chant before every bright outing.',
  say:'"My sun hat makes my own shade — brim over my face, strap under my chin, and my head stays cool even in the sun. Hat on before we go out, hat stays on while we play, water bottle close, and shade for our rests. That is how we do bright days."',
  notice:'On this card a playground supervisor with dark curly hair in a yellow polo shirt and a name-tag lanyard: picks a blue sun hat from a wooden shelf of hats in yellow, pink, green and beige, places it on her head at the playground, checks the fit with both hands — one at the brim, one at the chin strap — sits on a bench under a big blue shade sail, drinks from a blue water bottle with one eye happily scrunched, and holds a white thermometer reading 26°C.',
  ask:'"What does the brim do?" "How do we check the fit?" "Who picks your hat — and where does it live?" "What two friends come with the hat on hot days?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "My hat makes shade — feel it, cool!" run the fit check aloud: "Brim level, strap snug," or chant the three at the door: "Hat, bottle, shade!"',
  real:'Visit the hat shelf together — two choices, child picks, done. Notice the shade-workers at the park: the sail over the sandpit, the canopy over the swings, every good bench angled to the trees. Watch who else wears brims: gardeners, builders, beach lifeguards, the whole outdoor-professional crowd. That is the hat’s reputation talking.',
  play:'Shade-mapping in the garden or park: where does the hat’s shadow fall — draw it in chalk. Hat parade: decorate the chosen hat with the child’s name or patches. The strap-jump test as a game. And the routine rehearsal: hat on, bottle filled, shade spot picked — then out, with the felt-cool check one more time so the skin signs off.',
  tips:'Two hats double the odds: one by the door, one in the bag. Let the child pick the hat from a limited menu — a hat they chose is a hat they wear. And model it loudly: the grown-up hat goes on first, every time; the routine survives on visible grown-up compliance.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the brim make? How snug is the strap? What comes with the hat on hot days?" Shade, one-finger-snug, and water-and-shade — the hat is now a habit, not a fight.',
  safety:'Sun-hat safety: brim level so it shades, not blocks, the view; strap snug but never a loose cord near climbing frames — check it against playground equipment; and the hat is one of the summer three: it works with water breaks and shade rests, not instead of them.'},
 'sunglasses':{
  title:'Sunglasses',one:'Dimmers for bright days — choosing, fitting and caring for the eyes’ own sun gear.',
  seoTitle:'Sunglasses Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Glare Talk and the Two-Hands Routine (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what sunglasses do for bright light, the two-hands-on routine, the mirror fit check, glare by the water and the never-stare rule.',
  intro:'This lesson completes the sun-safety set: what glare is and why eyes squint, the two-hands-on routine for real glasses, the mirror fit check, where glare is strongest — water, sand, snow — and the golden rule that stays true with or without glasses: never stare at the sun.',
  what:'Sunglasses are glasses with dark lenses that cut down bright light and glare. They come in every frame colour, in child sizes, and they live in a case or on a hook when the sun goes in.',
  mean:'Bright light bounces — off water, sand, snow, pale pavements — and that bounced glare is what makes eyes squint and water. Sunglasses dim the glare so eyes stay comfortable. They are comfort gear for bright days, and they work as the third member of the sun team: hat, shade, glasses.',
  know:'Your child needs three sunglasses ideas: sunglasses dim glare so eyes stay comfy in bright light; the routine is two hands, gently on, comfy check in the mirror; and the golden rule never changes — sunglasses or not, we never look straight at the sun.',
  why:'Sunglasses are a child’s first owned optical device — putting them on carefully, storing them safely, understanding what they do. The care routine (case, not pocket) and the honest physics (glare bounces) make this a small lesson with big cross-over: respect for tools, respect for eyes.',
  dev:'Two-handed fine-motor routine, tool care and storage, light-compare observation, the body-signal link (squinting means glare), and rule-keeping under temptation — the never-stare rule tested and held.',
  vocab:'sunglasses, glare, bright, lenses, dim, fit, case, squint',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Sunglasses are dimmers for our eyes on very bright days."\n2) Read the picture: the pool instructor in a navy polo picks red sunglasses from a display rack of yellow, red, blue, purple and green pairs, puts them on with both hands, adjusts the arms over her ears for fit, stands looking toward a shaded canopy by the pool, wears them with a straw sun hat, and drinks from a clear water bottle with the pool bright behind her.\n3) Teach the routine as three moves: BOTH HANDS (one each side), GENTLY ON, COMFY CHECK (mirror, straight, no pinch). Practise until it is smooth and slow — glasses are handled, never grabbed.\n4) Do the light-compare: bright room, then sunglasses on — how did the light change? That dimming is the glasses doing their job for the eyes.\n5) Set the golden rule in stone: sunglasses dim the glare, but they are never a licence to stare — the sun is a no-look, always, with or without glasses.',
  say:'"On very bright days we wear sunglasses — they turn the brightness down so our eyes stay comfy. Two hands, gently on, comfy check in the mirror. And the big rule never changes: we never look right at the sun, with or without glasses."',
  notice:'On this card a pool instructor with dark curly hair in a navy "pool instructor" polo and a whistle lanyard: chooses red sunglasses from a display rack holding yellow, red, blue, purple and green pairs, puts them on with both hands by the pool, adjusts the arms over her ears, stands in profile looking toward a blue shade canopy and bench, wears the red glasses under a straw sun hat while adjusting the brim, and drinks from a clear bottle with the sparkling pool and bunting behind her.',
  ask:'"What do sunglasses do for our eyes?" "Show me the two-hands routine." "Where does glare bounce the most?" "What is the golden rule about the sun?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Squinting means glare — glasses please!" run the routine solo: "Two hands, gently on, comfy check," or guard the rule: "Never stare — not even with glasses on!"',
  real:'Feel the glare by water, sand or fresh snow — bounce you can see. Spot who wears glasses for work: lifeguards, sailors, skiers, drivers — all glare-fighters. Let your child carry their own pair in its own case: tool care is the hidden curriculum of this lesson.',
  play:'Mirror-school for the two-hands routine until it is ballet. Light-compare lab: sunny window, glasses on-off — report the change. Glare hunt with hats on: which spot by the pond bounces hardest? Choose-your-pair from two options — then the care routine: case click, hook hang, never the pocket.',
  tips:'Let some children opt out happily — the hat and the shade carry the load, and glasses are a bonus layer, not a battle. Pick real-dim lenses over toy dress-up sets if real wearing is the goal. And rehearse the never-stare rule out loud with the glasses ON: dark lenses can make staring feel safe when it is not, and saying so beats hoping.',
  check:'Ask: "What do sunglasses dim? What is the handling routine? Does the never-stare rule change with glasses on?" Glare, two-hands-gently, and never-never — eyes officially equipped.',
  safety:'Sunglasses safety: two hands, gentle handling — grabbed glasses break and broken bits are eye-hazards; real-dim lenses, not clear dress-up toys, if comfort is the goal; cases protect lenses and eyes alike; and the golden rule stands over every other line in this lesson — never look straight at the sun, with or without any glasses at all.'},
 'scarf-and-gloves':{
  title:'Scarf and Gloves',one:'The neck-and-fingers team — the snug wrap, the mitten wiggle and the tucked-in-for-play rule.',
  seoTitle:'Scarf and Gloves Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Snug Wrap and Tucked-In Play Rule (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: why necks and fingers chill first, the scarf wrap on self and teddy, gloves versus mittens, the warm-finger wiggle test and the tucked-in-for-playground rule.',
  intro:'This lesson adds the neck-and-finger team to the cold-day kit: why those bits chill first, the scarf wrap that is snug but never tight, gloves-versus-mittens by feel, the warm-finger check — and the tuck-it-in rule that keeps scarves safe on climbing frames.',
  what:'A scarf is a long soft strip of fabric wrapped around the neck to hold warmth in. Gloves give every finger its own cosy sleeve; mittens keep all the fingers together in one warm pouch.',
  mean:'Necks and fingers are the body’s cold-out posts — thin skin, far from the warm middle, always exposed. That is why they feel the cold first and why the scarf-and-glove team matters: the scarf holds a pocket of warm air at the neck, and covered fingers keep their wiggle for playing.',
  know:'Your child needs three scarf-and-glove ideas: necks and fingers feel cold first, so they get dressed on purpose; the scarf wraps snug — never tight — with ends tucked in; and on playgrounds the rule is tucked or off, because loose ends can catch on slides and frames.',
  why:'This lesson teaches precision care: warm is not just more layers, it is the right layers on the bits that need them. The wrap on teddy then on self builds the skill gently; the mittens-versus-gloves choice gives the child a real decision; and the tuck-in rule folds safety into the garment itself.',
  dev:'Fine-motor wrapping and tucking, body-map awareness (which bits chill first), choice-making (mittens or gloves today?), caring-for-others practice via teddy, and safety reasoning about loose ends and equipment.',
  vocab:'scarf, wrap, snug, gloves, mittens, fingers, tuck, warm',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Necks and fingers get cold first — so we dress them on purpose."\n2) Read the picture: the park ranger in a green hat and jacket picks a yellow scarf from a rack of purple, blue and red scarves, wraps it around her neck in the snowy park, pulls on a brown glove, wiggles both gloved hands warm, zips her jacket over the scarf and points at a forecast reading minus 4 degrees.\n3) Learn the wrap in two steps: around the neck, then tuck the ends in — snug enough to feel cosy, loose enough for one finger underneath. Practise on yourself, then on teddy (teddy sits very still and appreciates everything).\n4) Do the mitten-versus-glove try-on: which lets your fingers wiggle apart? Which feels warmer right now? Let your child choose by feel — both are right.\n5) Set the play rule like a rhyme: "Tucked or off for the slide and the climb" — loose, long ends stay home or get tucked before any playground gate.',
  say:'"On cold days our neck and fingers feel the cold first, so we wrap our scarf snug — around, then tuck the ends in — and pull on our gloves or mittens. Wiggle test! And when we play on the climbing frames: tucked or off, because loose ends catch."',
  notice:'On this card a park ranger in a green wide-brim hat with a badge and a dark green jacket stands in a snowy national park with pines and a park sign: she picks a yellow scarf from a wooden rack of colourful scarves, wraps the yellow scarf snugly around her neck with both hands, pulls a brown glove onto one hand, holds up both brown gloves with fingers spread, fastens her jacket zipper over the scarf, and points at a tablet reading −4°C with cloud and snowflake icons.',
  ask:'"Which bits feel cold first — and why?" "Show me the snug wrap." "Mittens or gloves today — and how do they feel different?" "What is the playground rule for scarves?"',
  childSay:'Your child might report: "Snug but one finger loose — perfect!" announce the choice: "Mittens today — warmest!" or recite the rhyme: "Tucked or off for the slide!"',
  real:'Watch the winter workers: every builder, ranger and dog walker has the neck-and-finger team on. Try the one-hand-in-cold-air test on the walk: gloved hand and bare hand, same wind — which taps the fence longer? Clip or string your child’s mittens to the sleeves and never chase a lost one again.',
  play:'Teddy-wrapping school: the wrap, the tuck, the praise. Mitten race: hands in, thumbs out, first up wins — then reversed with the non-favourite hand. Warm-finger science: bare-hand versus gloved-hand in the cold air, timed by who gives up first. And the rhyme-run at every playground gate: "Tucked or off!" before the first climb.',
  tips:'Buy duplicates of the mitten your child loves — the lost-mitten tax is real and pre-paying it preserves peace. Let the choice live with the child (mittens or gloves, scarf colour, wrap style) inside the two rules: snug-not-tight, tucked-or-off. And wrap teddy first, always: the skill lands softer through paws.',
  check:'Ask: "Which bits chill first? How snug is the wrap? What happens to scarf ends at the playground?" Neck-and-fingers, one-finger-loose, and tucked-or-off — the team is now on duty.',
  safety:'Scarf and glove safety: snug, never tight — one finger must fit under the wrap; ends tucked in, and on slides or climbing frames the rule is tucked or off entirely; no long cords attaching mittens through coat hoods or car seats; and wet mittens get swapped for dry at the radiator, not endured.'},
 'winter-coat':{
  title:'Winter Coat',one:'The big warm boss of cold days — the flip trick, the bottom-up zip and the car-seat rule.',
  seoTitle:'Winter Coat Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Flip Trick, Zipping and the Car-Seat Rule (Class 34)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how a winter coat holds warmth, the flip-over trick, zipping from the bottom tooth, the hood-and-scarf layering and the car-seat coat rule families must know.',
  intro:'This lesson crowns the winter kit with its boss level: how a padded coat actually works, the flip-over trick that ends doorway battles, zipping from the very bottom tooth, layering the hood and scarf like the card — and the car-seat rule every family needs before the first frosty drive.',
  what:'A winter coat is the warmest coat in the wardrobe: thick padding that traps a layer of warm air around the body, a cosy lining, deep pockets and usually a fur-trimmed hood for snowy gusts.',
  mean:'The coat works by trapping: the padding holds a blanket of still, warm air close to the body, and the shell keeps wind out of it. Fastened properly — zipped from the bottom up — it keeps that blanket sealed. Over clothes it is the outer layer; under it, jumpers add more trapped-air blankets.',
  know:'Your child needs three winter-coat ideas: the coat holds our warmth like a sleeping bag we wear; the skills are the flip trick and the bottom-up zip — both learnable, both celebratory; and the car rule is big and non-negotiable: bulky coats come OFF in the car seat, with a blanket over the straps instead.',
  why:'The winter coat is the Everest of self-dressing — big, stiff, long-zipped — which is exactly why mastering it matters so much. The flip trick gives a four-year-old a genuine technique, the solo zip gives genuine pride, and the car-seat rule gives the child words for a safety rule that even some grown-ups miss.',
  dev:'Big-garment motor planning (the flip!), multi-step zipping strength and control, layer-order reasoning (coat, then scarf, then gloves), safety rule ownership, and the confidence shift from dressed-by-someone to dressed-by-me.',
  vocab:'winter coat, padding, zip, flip trick, hood, layers, fasten, car seat',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "The winter coat is a sleeping bag you wear — it holds our warmth close."\n2) Read the picture: the bus driver at a snowy school-bus stop lifts the blue padded coat from its hanger, slips it on over her uniform, zips it to the top, pulls the fur-trimmed hood up, adds a red scarf as a warm middle layer, and holds up a white thermometer to check the cold.\n3) Learn the flip trick: coat on the floor, hood at your feet, arms into the holes, flip it up overhead — popcorn! The coat lands on your shoulders by itself. Practise until it is a party trick.\n4) Master the zip from the BOTTOM: bottom teeth together, slider down low, hold the bottom with one hand, zip up slow. The bottom-up habit is what makes long coats zippable at four.\n5) Teach the car rule with the coat itself: in the car seat, the big coat comes off — padding makes the straps sit too loose to hold us — arms out of the coat, into the seat, straps snug, then blanket OVER the straps. Practise it once in the garage so the real trips run smooth.',
  say:'"My winter coat holds my warmth close like a walking sleeping bag. I flip it on — popcorn! — then zip it from the bottom tooth all the way up. Hood up if it snows, scarf in the middle for extra warmth. And in the car: big coat off, straps snug, blanket on top — that is how warm and safe ride together."',
  notice:'The winter-coat card shows a bus driver in her navy uniform cap marked BUS, white shirt and tie, at a snowy stop before a yellow school bus and a blue bus-stop sign: she lifts the blue padded coat with its fur-trimmed hood from a hanger, slips it on over her uniform, fastens the zip to the collar, pulls the fur-trimmed hood up over the cap, wraps a red scarf around her neck as a middle layer, and holds a white thermometer to check how cold it is.',
  ask:'"Show me the flip trick — where does the hood start?" "Where does the zip begin — top or bottom?" "What goes over the coat for extra warmth?" "What happens to the big coat in the car seat?"',
  childSay:'Your child might shout the move: "Popcorn!" mid-flip, narrate the zip: "Bottom tooth… slider… up we go!" or own the car rule: "Coat off, straps snug, blanket on top — I know why!"',
  real:'Watch the flip-trick crowd at preschool drop-off — the coat-flyers versus the arm-wrestlers. Check the coat before the season: zip glides? hood turns the head? cuffs still snug? Do a parked-car rehearsal of the coat-off-straps-snug-blanket-on routine once, calmly, before it matters in the morning rush.',
  play:'Popcorn-practice until it is show-off material — then the coat-flip demo for the whole family. Zip-ladder: from the bottom tooth, one tooth at a time, celebrating halfway and summiting. Kit-order line-up: coat, scarf, gloves laid out in wearing order by the child. Car-seat drill as a teddy lesson: teddy’s big coat off, straps snug, blanket over — "because padding hides the snug."',
  tips:'The flip trick is the single best gift in this lesson — ten patient minutes of practice buys years of doorway peace. If zipping stalls, share the job: grown-up starts the slider at the bottom, child runs it to the top; finishers come back tomorrow. And say the WHY of the car rule out loud once, clearly: padding hides how snug the straps really are — the blanket keeps the warmth without lying to the straps.',
  check:'Ask: "Show me the flip trick. Where does the zip start? What is the car-seat coat rule?" Popcorn, bottom-tooth, and coat-off-blanket-over — the boss level is cleared, and the child knows it.',
  safety:'Winter-coat safety, in order: the car-seat rule comes first — bulky coats off in the seat, straps snug against clothes, blanket over the straps; zips checked for chin-catch and smooth glide; hoods down indoors where they block the view and snag on hooks; and a properly fastened coat beats an open one every frosty day — the trapped warm air only works when it is sealed.'}
};
